WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.465 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.465 --> 00:00:04.437 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:06.902 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:06.902 --> 00:00:17.570 align:middle line:90%


00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:21.260 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: OK, let me
go ahead and get started.

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:24.870 align:middle line:84%
Today's topic is
going to be entry.

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:27.660 align:middle line:84%
And I think entry is an
incredibly important topic

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:28.160 align:middle line:90%
in IO.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.159 align:middle line:84%
In fact, one of the
messages I'm going

00:00:30.159 --> 00:00:32.659 align:middle line:84%
to talk about today is, in some
sense, just about everything

00:00:32.659 --> 00:00:34.190 align:middle line:84%
we've done in this
semester so far

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:37.080 align:middle line:84%
is kind of like rounding error
compared to what we do now.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:42.410 align:middle line:90%
So how do I think about entry?

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:44.870 align:middle line:84%
So far, we've talked
about a lot of models that

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:47.540 align:middle line:90%
have the number of firms fixed.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:49.040 align:middle line:84%
And I've talked
about lots of things

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.273 align:middle line:84%
about factors that
affect pricing

00:00:51.273 --> 00:00:52.565 align:middle line:90%
with the number of firms fixed.

00:00:52.565 --> 00:00:54.773 align:middle line:84%
You know, monopoly pricing,
if you have one firm, how

00:00:54.773 --> 00:00:57.030 align:middle line:90%
does that-- what do they do?

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:00.920 align:middle line:84%
And then static competition
with vertical differentiation

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:03.873 align:middle line:84%
or horizontal differentiation
or search costs or other things,

00:01:03.873 --> 00:01:05.540 align:middle line:84%
and it's always the
nature of the market

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:07.940 align:middle line:84%
is determining what's
the level of markups

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:11.710 align:middle line:84%
and what inefficiencies
there might be.

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:14.540 align:middle line:84%
But that's all taking
market structure as fixed.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:16.390 align:middle line:84%
And so starting today,
I'm going to think

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:20.298 align:middle line:84%
about what happens if you allow
the number of firms to change.

00:01:20.298 --> 00:01:22.090 align:middle line:84%
And so the simplest
way to think about this

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:25.240 align:middle line:84%
is let's look at
a two-stage model.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:27.820 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to start with the
case of homogeneous goods.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:31.090 align:middle line:84%
So you can think of this
as old-fashioned steel

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:34.000 align:middle line:84%
plants entering in each--
producing cold-rolled steel

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.180 align:middle line:84%
or aluminum plants
or some industry that

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:39.700 align:middle line:84%
has a small number of
firms because they're

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:42.010 align:middle line:84%
big fixed costs of
setting up plants

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:43.780 align:middle line:84%
and then they compete
with each other.

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:47.570 align:middle line:84%
So stage one, we have a large
number of potential entrants.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:49.880 align:middle line:84%
The potential entrants
choose in or out.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:54.490 align:middle line:84%
If they decide to come in, they
pay some fixed entry cost K.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:56.800 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to think about
this, most of the lecture,

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:57.803 align:middle line:90%
as a sunk cost.

00:01:57.803 --> 00:02:00.220 align:middle line:84%
That is, once you build a steel
plant, it's a steel plant,

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:01.940 align:middle line:84%
you can't use it
for anything else.

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:04.148 align:middle line:84%
You're not going be able to
sell it to somebody else,

00:02:04.148 --> 00:02:06.340 align:middle line:90%
do something else with it.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.830 align:middle line:84%
Firms that choose to
enter then play some game

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:12.170 align:middle line:84%
like Bertrand competition,
Cournot competition, price

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:14.220 align:middle line:84%
competition with
search, et cetera.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:18.370 align:middle line:84%
And assume firms have some
variable costs c of Q.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:20.370 align:middle line:84%
And then, how are you
going to solve this model?

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:23.210 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to solve this
model by backward induction.

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:25.410 align:middle line:84%
I first start with
the second stage.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:28.943 align:middle line:84%
In the second stage,
if there are N firms,

00:02:28.943 --> 00:02:30.860 align:middle line:84%
there are going to be
some equilibrium prices,

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:36.870 align:middle line:84%
p star of N. p star of N times Q
star of N minus c of Q star of N

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:38.870 align:middle line:84%
is going to be the variable
profits of the firms

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:39.990 align:middle line:90%
if they enter.

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:42.440 align:middle line:84%
And then you find the number
of equilibrium entrants

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:43.735 align:middle line:90%
by looking at the first stage.

00:02:43.735 --> 00:02:46.604 align:middle line:90%


00:02:46.604 --> 00:02:49.970 align:middle line:90%
And what's that going to be?

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:52.700 align:middle line:84%
OK, first comment, I guess
these slides were made up

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:57.980 align:middle line:84%
in the COVID era, where I would
write on them right on my iPad.

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:00.180 align:middle line:84%
But let me go ahead and
do this on the board.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:09.020 align:middle line:84%
So let me graph what our
profits look like as a function

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:10.170 align:middle line:90%
of the number of firms.

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:14.540 align:middle line:84%
The easiest one to
graph is Bertrand.

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:20.030 align:middle line:90%
So 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:27.200 align:middle line:84%
Bertrand competition, you have
this level of profits, pi M,

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:29.300 align:middle line:90%
if you have one firm.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:36.220 align:middle line:84%
And then as soon as you have
two firms, profits drop to 0.

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:39.610 align:middle line:84%
Another thing you could
have is Cournot competition.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:43.060 align:middle line:84%
If you have Cournot competition,
you get exactly the same outcome

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:46.060 align:middle line:90%
when N equals 1.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:50.390 align:middle line:84%
But then profits
drop when you add

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:55.490 align:middle line:90%
2, 3, 4, or 5, whatever firms.

00:03:55.490 --> 00:03:58.640 align:middle line:84%
If you think about-- if we did
Cournot with linear demand--

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:00.200 align:middle line:84%
Cournot with linear
demands, you were

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:06.440 align:middle line:84%
getting, like, P star is
approximately c plus 1 over--

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:12.480 align:middle line:84%
Or simplest case--
simplest case for Cournot

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:17.550 align:middle line:84%
was N firms demand
P as 1 over P.

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:20.730 align:middle line:84%
And then I think in that model,
it was P star was equal to 1

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:22.110 align:middle line:90%
over N plus 1.

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:25.530 align:middle line:84%
And q star was equal
to 1 over N plus 1.

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:28.850 align:middle line:84%
So profits were like
1 over N squared.

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:32.360 align:middle line:84%
So Cournot profits,
approximately 1

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:35.545 align:middle line:84%
over N squared or 1 over N
plus 1 squared, whatever.

00:04:35.545 --> 00:04:38.810 align:middle line:90%


00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:44.390 align:middle line:84%
Another model I did was
a model with search.

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:46.370 align:middle line:84%
In a model with search,
if you remember,

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:48.230 align:middle line:84%
there was the Stahl
model, you would always

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:50.150 align:middle line:84%
have the upper bound
of the distribution,

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:52.420 align:middle line:84%
always gave you the
monopoly profits

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:55.220 align:middle line:84%
as long as the search costs
were in an appropriate range.

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:58.560 align:middle line:84%
And so that one
declined something--

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:00.110 align:middle line:84%
if you think about
that, that means

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:03.750 align:middle line:84%
it declines slower
because the firms are

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:06.870 align:middle line:90%
serving the nonshoppers.

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:09.420 align:middle line:84%
So in the search model, you're
serving the nonshoppers.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:11.860 align:middle line:84%
You're always indifferent to
charging the monopoly price.

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:13.235 align:middle line:84%
And so you get
the monopoly price

00:05:13.235 --> 00:05:17.490 align:middle line:84%
divided by N plus-- divided by N
times 1 minus mu is the profits.

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:23.100 align:middle line:84%
And they go down like 1 over
N. But whatever you have,

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:26.140 align:middle line:84%
what's going to happen in this
model in the first stage--

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:28.830 align:middle line:84%
so if you have some
level of fixed--

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:31.020 align:middle line:90%
let me focus on Cournot.

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:36.450 align:middle line:84%
Let's suppose you do Cournot,
and you have some fixed level

00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:40.320 align:middle line:84%
of entry cost K. Then,
in equilibrium, you're

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:42.840 align:middle line:84%
going to have exactly
two firms enter.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:45.810 align:middle line:84%
Because when two firms
enter, the firms' profits

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:48.720 align:middle line:84%
are above K. If you had
three firms in the market,

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:51.690 align:middle line:84%
the profits would be
below K, so N equals 2.

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:56.070 align:middle line:84%
And so the number of firms,
it's locally invariant to K

00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:59.100 align:middle line:84%
because you can
raise or lower costs,

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:00.340 align:middle line:90%
and it doesn't affect things.

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:04.792 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, if K drops,
then the third firm enters.

00:06:04.792 --> 00:06:10.500 align:middle line:84%
(That's a terrible
piece of chalk.)

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:14.440 align:middle line:84%
You could also think of a mixed
strategy equilibrium solution.

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:17.410 align:middle line:84%
If there's some large
but finite number--

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:24.200 align:middle line:84%
if there's some number of
M of potential entrants,

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:28.010 align:middle line:84%
where M is much
bigger than N star--

00:06:28.010 --> 00:06:31.940 align:middle line:84%
you could also think of M
firms each mixing in or out,

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:34.400 align:middle line:84%
and then you get a mixed
strategy equilibrium.

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:36.205 align:middle line:84%
In the mixed
strategy equilibrium,

00:06:36.205 --> 00:06:37.580 align:middle line:84%
all the firms that
are mixing are

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:40.980 align:middle line:84%
going to have to have expected
profits exactly equal to K.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:44.090 align:middle line:84%
And so in that model, all
the firms that are mixing

00:06:44.090 --> 00:06:49.450 align:middle line:84%
have expected profits exactly
equal to K. Therefore,

00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:51.490 align:middle line:84%
here you can think
of it as you're

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:54.570 align:middle line:84%
going to expect to get
two-plus firms, between two

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:55.820 align:middle line:90%
and three firms in the market.

00:06:55.820 --> 00:06:58.450 align:middle line:84%
But if it's-- obviously,
if it's like Poisson draws,

00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:03.370 align:middle line:84%
sometimes you'll get one, two,
three, four, five, or more.

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:06.023 align:middle line:84%
In models like a
procurement auction,

00:07:06.023 --> 00:07:07.690 align:middle line:84%
where there's one
date on which everyone

00:07:07.690 --> 00:07:09.940 align:middle line:84%
has to submit sealed
bids and no one knows

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:14.260 align:middle line:84%
how many other bidders there
are, this expected pi of N

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:16.550 align:middle line:84%
equals K is probably
the right approach.

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:19.960 align:middle line:84%
If you have models
like the steel plants

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:22.390 align:middle line:84%
where the firms are not
entering simultaneously,

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:24.130 align:middle line:84%
they're entering
sequentially, then

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:26.320 align:middle line:84%
it's probably like the
pure strategy equilibrium

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:28.570 align:middle line:84%
is the one you want to focus
on because no one's going

00:07:28.570 --> 00:07:30.820 align:middle line:84%
to open a third steel plant if
the third steel plant is going

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:33.403 align:middle line:84%
to drive prices below where you
need to cover the fixed costs.

00:07:33.403 --> 00:07:39.240 align:middle line:90%


00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:44.310 align:middle line:84%
So observations-- fixed
costs and competition

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:46.930 align:middle line:84%
determine the number of
entries, number of entrants.

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:51.580 align:middle line:84%
But with lower fixed
costs, we get more entry.

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:53.680 align:middle line:84%
In many models,
the number of firms

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:57.260 align:middle line:84%
goes to infinity as the
fixed costs of entry go to 0.

00:07:57.260 --> 00:08:01.690 align:middle line:84%
The only time that's not going
to happen is if you had--

00:08:01.690 --> 00:08:03.370 align:middle line:84%
I talked about the
low demand models,

00:08:03.370 --> 00:08:07.870 align:middle line:84%
where price asymptotes
to a positive--

00:08:07.870 --> 00:08:09.820 align:middle line:90%
no, it's still going to work.

00:08:09.820 --> 00:08:11.540 align:middle line:84%
So where are you not
going to N star--

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:16.118 align:middle line:90%


00:08:16.118 --> 00:08:18.410 align:middle line:84%
You're not going to get N
star goes to infinity-- like,

00:08:18.410 --> 00:08:20.000 align:middle line:84%
in Cournot, you
don't get N star goes

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.590 align:middle line:84%
to infinity because in Cournot,
in fact, you get N star is 1.

00:08:22.590 --> 00:08:27.020 align:middle line:84%
So if profits hit 0 at some
point before, when N gets large,

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:29.550 align:middle line:84%
and even with no entry
costs, you can still get--

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:34.280 align:middle line:84%
or small positive entry costs,
you can still get finite entry.

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:37.830 align:middle line:84%
Profits are, in this model,
are a rounding error.

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:39.960 align:middle line:84%
And we spent all this
time working on markups,

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:46.580 align:middle line:84%
but here, the profits
are just-- if this

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:49.980 align:middle line:84%
happens to be the fixed cost,
you get pretty big profits.

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:52.800 align:middle line:84%
If this was the fixed cost,
you'd get very little profits.

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:58.070 align:middle line:84%
If the fixed costs dropped here,
you'd get even higher profits.

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:01.820 align:middle line:84%
When then they drop more, you
get hardly any profits at all.

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:07.028 align:middle line:84%
So equilibrium profits
that we observed really

00:09:07.028 --> 00:09:09.320 align:middle line:84%
don't have to be related to
anything we've talked about

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:11.960 align:middle line:84%
so far because all that
matters is how many firms

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:12.980 align:middle line:90%
fit in the market.

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:15.370 align:middle line:84%
And then everything
else just determines

00:09:15.370 --> 00:09:17.870 align:middle line:84%
where you are in between the
profits with two firms or three

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:19.920 align:middle line:84%
firms or three firms
and four firms.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:22.050 align:middle line:84%
And so profits just
become this rounding error

00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:24.830 align:middle line:84%
that's just coincidence of
where entry costs big enough

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:27.330 align:middle line:84%
that the last firm
just barely gets in.

00:09:27.330 --> 00:09:34.260 align:middle line:84%
Now, that's some-- that is
equilibrium profits taking

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:37.170 align:middle line:84%
the fixed costs of
entry into account.

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:38.670 align:middle line:84%
It's still the case
that the markups

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:40.818 align:middle line:84%
are higher when you have
smaller numbers of firms.

00:09:40.818 --> 00:09:42.360 align:middle line:84%
The markups are
higher, but it's just

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:43.950 align:middle line:84%
the markups just
offset the fixed

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:48.150 align:middle line:90%
costs the firms are incurring.

00:09:48.150 --> 00:09:51.510 align:middle line:84%
In many models, the
equilibrium firm size

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:53.680 align:middle line:84%
increases when the
market size increases.

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:57.150 align:middle line:84%
So I give the
Cournot example here.

00:09:57.150 --> 00:09:59.010 align:middle line:84%
In the Cournot
example, suppose you

00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:01.800 align:middle line:84%
have little m consumers
in the market,

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:05.040 align:middle line:84%
and so demand becomes
m times D of p.

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:07.260 align:middle line:84%
Let's suppose we
double the market size.

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:09.270 align:middle line:84%
If you double the
market size and you

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:14.250 align:middle line:84%
double the number of firms, then
the number of customers per firm

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:15.790 align:middle line:90%
would be the same.

00:10:15.790 --> 00:10:19.570 align:middle line:84%
But there's a second effect
of the prices decreasing.

00:10:19.570 --> 00:10:22.920 align:middle line:90%
And so if the prices decrease--

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:25.830 align:middle line:84%
the prices decrease, then
the firms are worse off.

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:28.920 align:middle line:84%
So Cournot, in some sense, it's
like the profits are going down

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:30.600 align:middle line:90%
like 1 over N squared.

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:33.870 align:middle line:84%
With N firms, profits
are going up proportional

00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:36.250 align:middle line:84%
to m if the number
of customers goes up.

00:10:36.250 --> 00:10:38.060 align:middle line:90%
So you're going to get--

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:39.810 align:middle line:84%
firm growth is going
to be the square root

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:41.440 align:middle line:90%
of the number of customers.

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:42.810 align:middle line:90%
So as the firm--

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:45.420 align:middle line:84%
as the market
increases, firms go up

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:47.500 align:middle line:84%
square root of the number
of customers goes up.

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:50.650 align:middle line:84%
So firm size goes up like square
root of the number of customers.

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:55.090 align:middle line:90%


00:10:55.090 --> 00:10:57.486 align:middle line:84%
Any questions on these
simple observations?

00:10:57.486 --> 00:11:01.510 align:middle line:90%


00:11:01.510 --> 00:11:04.120 align:middle line:84%
First substantial topic I
want to talk about today

00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:08.120 align:middle line:84%
is, what's the
efficiency of entry?

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:10.520 align:middle line:84%
Do we get more firms and
markets than we want?

00:11:10.520 --> 00:11:12.770 align:middle line:84%
Do we get fewer firms
and markets than we want?

00:11:12.770 --> 00:11:15.010 align:middle line:84%
And this is an important
practical question.

00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:17.500 align:middle line:84%
There are many, many industries
and many, many countries

00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:19.000 align:middle line:84%
where the firms
have gotten together

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:22.630 align:middle line:84%
and have some kind of
government-sanctioned entry

00:11:22.630 --> 00:11:24.940 align:middle line:90%
limitations.

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:27.370 align:middle line:84%
There's a question on the
Massachusetts ballot about

00:11:27.370 --> 00:11:30.520 align:middle line:90%
should liquor stores--

00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:34.960 align:middle line:84%
or should firms be able to sell
beer in more than nine stores

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:36.225 align:middle line:90%
in the state of Massachusetts?

00:11:36.225 --> 00:11:38.350 align:middle line:84%
For many years, you were
only allowed to sell beer,

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:40.520 align:middle line:84%
I think, in one store in
the state of Massachusetts.

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:42.790 align:middle line:84%
So all the supermarkets,
you notice--

00:11:42.790 --> 00:11:43.810 align:middle line:90%
I don't know if--

00:11:43.810 --> 00:11:47.555 align:middle line:84%
if you go to-- supermarkets
in other states have beer.

00:11:47.555 --> 00:11:49.180 align:middle line:84%
Supermarkets in
Massachusetts typically

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:50.597 align:middle line:84%
didn't have beer
because there was

00:11:50.597 --> 00:11:54.430 align:middle line:84%
a law that said you could only
sell beer in one of your stores

00:11:54.430 --> 00:11:56.000 align:middle line:84%
in the entire state
of Massachusetts.

00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:00.060 align:middle line:84%
And so Star Market had to pick
one Star Market to have beer.

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:01.310 align:middle line:90%
And now there are--

00:12:01.310 --> 00:12:03.123 align:middle line:84%
now you can sell beer
in nine Star Markets,

00:12:03.123 --> 00:12:05.040 align:middle line:84%
and they're thinking of
increasing that level.

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:10.217 align:middle line:84%
But why do we get
these entry laws?

00:12:10.217 --> 00:12:11.800 align:middle line:84%
Well, the firms that
are in the market

00:12:11.800 --> 00:12:15.610 align:middle line:84%
always argue that competition
entry is wasteful,

00:12:15.610 --> 00:12:17.298 align:middle line:84%
and we need to save
the mom and pop

00:12:17.298 --> 00:12:19.840 align:middle line:84%
stores and the small businesses
and protect them from, quote,

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:21.090 align:middle line:90%
excessive entry.

00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:25.390 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, here's a discussion
of do we think entry should

00:12:25.390 --> 00:12:29.320 align:middle line:84%
or shouldn't, would or would
not be excessive in markets?

00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:31.150 align:middle line:90%
What's the first best?

00:12:31.150 --> 00:12:33.880 align:middle line:84%
Here I'm going to do the
homogeneous case first.

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:37.880 align:middle line:84%
Homogeneous goods, there's
no idiosyncratic preferences.

00:12:37.880 --> 00:12:39.610 align:middle line:84%
So the first best
is just one firm--

00:12:39.610 --> 00:12:42.760 align:middle line:84%
have that one firm
price at marginal cost.

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:44.620 align:middle line:84%
That's typically not
feasible because you'd

00:12:44.620 --> 00:12:46.870 align:middle line:84%
need price regulation to
force the monopolist to price

00:12:46.870 --> 00:12:47.660 align:middle line:90%
at marginal cost.

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:50.140 align:middle line:84%
So what we often compare
it to is the second best,

00:12:50.140 --> 00:12:53.990 align:middle line:84%
where the second best you
take p star of N as given.

00:12:53.990 --> 00:12:56.277 align:middle line:84%
You can't affect the
nature of the competition,

00:12:56.277 --> 00:12:58.360 align:middle line:84%
and you just say, what
would be the optimal number

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:02.470 align:middle line:84%
of firms, given
that p star of N can

00:13:02.470 --> 00:13:04.150 align:middle line:90%
be determined in equilibrium?

00:13:04.150 --> 00:13:08.830 align:middle line:84%
And so this would be
our welfare calculation.

00:13:08.830 --> 00:13:15.700 align:middle line:84%
The welfare with N firms is
equal to the integral 0 to N--

00:13:15.700 --> 00:13:17.350 align:middle line:90%
did we do big Q or little q?

00:13:17.350 --> 00:13:17.895 align:middle line:90%
Little q.

00:13:17.895 --> 00:13:33.250 align:middle line:90%


00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:37.450 align:middle line:84%
So if you have N firms, this
is the gross surplus produced

00:13:37.450 --> 00:13:43.390 align:middle line:84%
by firms producing total
quantity N q star of N. This

00:13:43.390 --> 00:13:46.330 align:middle line:84%
is the total production costs
incurred when each of N firms

00:13:46.330 --> 00:13:50.620 align:middle line:84%
produces quantity q star of N.
And these are the fixed costs.

00:13:50.620 --> 00:13:53.080 align:middle line:90%
So we want to maximize that.

00:13:53.080 --> 00:13:56.410 align:middle line:84%
So the first theorem
is that in this model

00:13:56.410 --> 00:13:59.510 align:middle line:84%
with homogeneous goods,
entry is always excessive.

00:13:59.510 --> 00:14:01.900 align:middle line:84%
So we do get too many
firms and markets.

00:14:01.900 --> 00:14:03.200 align:middle line:90%
We would like to restrict it.

00:14:03.200 --> 00:14:03.880 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:05.320 align:middle line:90%
Here's a theorem.

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:09.423 align:middle line:84%
So suppose that this
welfare function is concave.

00:14:09.423 --> 00:14:11.840 align:middle line:84%
I know I've talked about how
things are often not concave.

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:13.540 align:middle line:84%
Welfare functions
here would be concave

00:14:13.540 --> 00:14:17.110 align:middle line:84%
because as you
get lots of firms,

00:14:17.110 --> 00:14:20.983 align:middle line:84%
you just asymptote to
line of slope K. It's

00:14:20.983 --> 00:14:23.650 align:middle line:84%
inefficient to keep paying these
fixed entry costs over and over

00:14:23.650 --> 00:14:25.360 align:middle line:90%
and over again.

00:14:25.360 --> 00:14:28.280 align:middle line:84%
So suppose the welfare
function is concave.

00:14:28.280 --> 00:14:30.700 align:middle line:84%
Suppose the variable
profits are decreasing

00:14:30.700 --> 00:14:34.500 align:middle line:84%
in number of firms
N. And suppose

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:38.070 align:middle line:84%
that the equilibrium
price per firm quantities

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:40.380 align:middle line:84%
can be extended to
differentiable functions that

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:42.660 align:middle line:90%
have these natural properties.

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:47.890 align:middle line:84%
As the number of firms
increases, total output goes up.

00:14:47.890 --> 00:14:51.250 align:middle line:84%
As the number of firms goes up,
each individual firm's output

00:14:51.250 --> 00:14:53.070 align:middle line:90%
goes down.

00:14:53.070 --> 00:15:01.060 align:middle line:84%
And at the equilibrium number of
firms, price minus marginal cost

00:15:01.060 --> 00:15:05.310 align:middle line:84%
is positive, which
it always should be.

00:15:05.310 --> 00:15:07.060 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, this is given
any number of firms--

00:15:07.060 --> 00:15:09.970 align:middle line:84%
given any number of firms
N, the resulting price

00:15:09.970 --> 00:15:12.130 align:middle line:90%
would be above marginal cost.

00:15:12.130 --> 00:15:16.880 align:middle line:84%
The theorem is that then N is
at least N second best minus 1.

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:18.970 align:middle line:84%
So it's entries
excessive, although it

00:15:18.970 --> 00:15:21.700 align:middle line:84%
can, due to rounding
errors, be one too few.

00:15:21.700 --> 00:15:24.110 align:middle line:84%
Bertrand is a case
where it's one too few.

00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:27.430 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes you'd like two
firms, you only get one.

00:15:27.430 --> 00:15:29.650 align:middle line:84%
But the general
result is that you

00:15:29.650 --> 00:15:32.965 align:middle line:84%
get too many firms, other than
this possible rounding error

00:15:32.965 --> 00:15:33.465 align:middle line:90%
issue.

00:15:33.465 --> 00:15:36.960 align:middle line:90%


00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:39.030 align:middle line:90%
So proof.

00:15:39.030 --> 00:15:42.360 align:middle line:84%
So first, we've got
this welfare function.

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:44.910 align:middle line:84%
The welfare function is
naturally described only

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:46.400 align:middle line:90%
at a set of integer points.

00:15:46.400 --> 00:15:51.680 align:middle line:90%


00:15:51.680 --> 00:15:55.960 align:middle line:84%
But in many of these models,
like Cournot over there,

00:15:55.960 --> 00:16:05.500 align:middle line:84%
you have p star of N equals
1 over N plus 1, Q star of N

00:16:05.500 --> 00:16:07.710 align:middle line:90%
equals 1 over N plus 1.

00:16:07.710 --> 00:16:11.080 align:middle line:84%
So you can just naturally treat
this as a continuous function

00:16:11.080 --> 00:16:15.310 align:middle line:84%
when N is a continuous variable,
like use a function Q star of x

00:16:15.310 --> 00:16:17.240 align:middle line:90%
equals 1 over x plus 1.

00:16:17.240 --> 00:16:19.900 align:middle line:84%
And by doing that,
you extend the--

00:16:19.900 --> 00:16:24.150 align:middle line:84%
you just draw a smooth curve
through those welfare points.

00:16:24.150 --> 00:16:27.800 align:middle line:90%


00:16:27.800 --> 00:16:29.750 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to let
N hat second best

00:16:29.750 --> 00:16:32.480 align:middle line:84%
be the solution to where this
thing's first order condition is

00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:33.740 align:middle line:90%
0.

00:16:33.740 --> 00:16:35.175 align:middle line:90%
So that would be right here.

00:16:35.175 --> 00:16:39.062 align:middle line:90%


00:16:39.062 --> 00:16:40.860 align:middle line:90%
N hat second best.

00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:44.150 align:middle line:84%
So I define N hat second
best to be the maximizer

00:16:44.150 --> 00:16:45.115 align:middle line:90%
of this smooth curve.

00:16:45.115 --> 00:16:48.470 align:middle line:90%


00:16:48.470 --> 00:16:53.155 align:middle line:84%
The second best is always
going to be either--

00:16:53.155 --> 00:16:56.450 align:middle line:84%
if W is a concave
function, it's either

00:16:56.450 --> 00:16:58.900 align:middle line:84%
going to be this
point or this point.

00:16:58.900 --> 00:17:04.780 align:middle line:84%
So the optimal number of firms
is always at most what you get--

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:06.940 align:middle line:84%
optimal number first
is always at most

00:17:06.940 --> 00:17:12.010 align:middle line:84%
what you get by just rounding up
this point, N hat second best.

00:17:12.010 --> 00:17:14.319 align:middle line:84%
So what is the first
order condition

00:17:14.319 --> 00:17:16.060 align:middle line:90%
defining N hat second best?

00:17:16.060 --> 00:17:18.640 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's just-- the
first order condition

00:17:18.640 --> 00:17:31.290 align:middle line:84%
is d W of N dN equals evaluated
at N hat second best equals 0.

00:17:31.290 --> 00:17:35.410 align:middle line:84%
That's what determines
this point here.

00:17:35.410 --> 00:17:37.220 align:middle line:84%
What does that
derivative look like?

00:17:37.220 --> 00:17:39.940 align:middle line:84%
So I differentiate this
expression with respect

00:17:39.940 --> 00:17:42.640 align:middle line:84%
to N. The first
term only depends

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:46.300 align:middle line:84%
on N through the upper
limit of integration.

00:17:46.300 --> 00:17:49.360 align:middle line:84%
So the derivative of
this with respect to N

00:17:49.360 --> 00:17:53.080 align:middle line:84%
is just the value
of this evaluated

00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:56.890 align:middle line:84%
at N q star of N,
P of N q star of N

00:17:56.890 --> 00:17:59.830 align:middle line:84%
times the amount by which
the upper limit moves

00:17:59.830 --> 00:18:00.920 align:middle line:90%
when N changes.

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:03.610 align:middle line:84%
So the derivative of
this with respect to N

00:18:03.610 --> 00:18:05.100 align:middle line:90%
is what I wrote down here.

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:08.610 align:middle line:90%


00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:12.180 align:middle line:84%
The second effect
of N on welfare

00:18:12.180 --> 00:18:16.030 align:middle line:84%
is this term changes,
Nc of q star of N.

00:18:16.030 --> 00:18:18.690 align:middle line:84%
So the derivative of
that with respect to N

00:18:18.690 --> 00:18:25.080 align:middle line:84%
is c of q star of N plus
Nc prime of q star of N,

00:18:25.080 --> 00:18:31.252 align:middle line:84%
q star prime at N. So that's a
derivative of the second term.

00:18:31.252 --> 00:18:33.710 align:middle line:84%
And then the derivative of the
third term with respect to N

00:18:33.710 --> 00:18:36.330 align:middle line:90%
is just K.

00:18:36.330 --> 00:18:39.180 align:middle line:84%
So I just differentiate
this expression with respect

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:44.070 align:middle line:84%
to N. I say that evaluated
at N hat second best

00:18:44.070 --> 00:18:47.760 align:middle line:90%
is going to be equal to 0.

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:50.465 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm going to
start grouping terms.

00:18:50.465 --> 00:18:58.200 align:middle line:84%
The first term I pull together
are P times q minus c of q.

00:18:58.200 --> 00:19:02.250 align:middle line:84%
And so I'm going to take this
term times this minus this

00:19:02.250 --> 00:19:04.770 align:middle line:84%
and call that the
variable profit.

00:19:04.770 --> 00:19:08.670 align:middle line:90%
So that's pi v star of N.

00:19:08.670 --> 00:19:10.570 align:middle line:90%
What's left over?

00:19:10.570 --> 00:19:15.390 align:middle line:84%
Everything that's left over has
an Nq star prime of N. Well,

00:19:15.390 --> 00:19:16.690 align:middle line:90%
there are two terms left over.

00:19:16.690 --> 00:19:21.810 align:middle line:84%
This times this and this that
have an Nq star prime at N.

00:19:21.810 --> 00:19:25.200 align:middle line:84%
And those become Nq
star prime at N times

00:19:25.200 --> 00:19:28.300 align:middle line:90%
price minus marginal cost.

00:19:28.300 --> 00:19:31.110 align:middle line:84%
And then the third thing that's
left over is the K at the end.

00:19:31.110 --> 00:19:33.760 align:middle line:90%


00:19:33.760 --> 00:19:35.890 align:middle line:84%
And then under this
assumption that I

00:19:35.890 --> 00:19:40.390 align:middle line:84%
made that price
minus marginal cost

00:19:40.390 --> 00:19:43.020 align:middle line:84%
is always positive at
the equilibrium prices,

00:19:43.020 --> 00:19:45.540 align:middle line:90%
this is a positive number.

00:19:45.540 --> 00:19:52.400 align:middle line:84%
And so what that means is that
if you evaluate this at N hat

00:19:52.400 --> 00:20:03.580 align:middle line:84%
second best, I get variable
profit minus K plus this equals

00:20:03.580 --> 00:20:04.080 align:middle line:90%
0.

00:20:04.080 --> 00:20:07.340 align:middle line:84%
That tells me that the variable
profit evaluated at N second

00:20:07.340 --> 00:20:12.380 align:middle line:84%
best minus K is strictly
positive, which means that 1x--

00:20:12.380 --> 00:20:16.560 align:middle line:84%
at N hat second
best, variable profit

00:20:16.560 --> 00:20:19.710 align:middle line:84%
is bigger than K. So that
means other firms would

00:20:19.710 --> 00:20:21.270 align:middle line:90%
like to come in.

00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:24.000 align:middle line:84%
So if we had exactly
this many firms,

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:26.040 align:middle line:84%
not that this is an
integer, the next firm

00:20:26.040 --> 00:20:27.081 align:middle line:90%
always wants to come in.

00:20:27.081 --> 00:20:28.623 align:middle line:84%
So that means the
equilibrium entry--

00:20:28.623 --> 00:20:32.160 align:middle line:90%


00:20:32.160 --> 00:20:34.290 align:middle line:90%
oops.

00:20:34.290 --> 00:20:40.260 align:middle line:84%
So equilibrium entry
is at least here.

00:20:40.260 --> 00:20:43.200 align:middle line:84%
Because at this point, we
know a firm wants to come in.

00:20:43.200 --> 00:20:44.410 align:middle line:90%
At this point, we don't know.

00:20:44.410 --> 00:20:46.140 align:middle line:84%
But if you're less,
then you definitely

00:20:46.140 --> 00:20:49.390 align:middle line:84%
want to come in because pi v
star is a decreasing function.

00:20:49.390 --> 00:20:53.020 align:middle line:84%
So pi v star is bigger
than K at this point.

00:20:53.020 --> 00:20:55.870 align:middle line:84%
So pi v star is also bigger
than K at this point.

00:20:55.870 --> 00:20:59.765 align:middle line:90%
So N star is somewhere out here.

00:20:59.765 --> 00:21:02.480 align:middle line:90%


00:21:02.480 --> 00:21:04.760 align:middle line:84%
So N star is at
least what you get--

00:21:04.760 --> 00:21:06.650 align:middle line:90%
is at least here.

00:21:06.650 --> 00:21:07.610 align:middle line:90%
And what did I say?

00:21:07.610 --> 00:21:14.850 align:middle line:84%
I said that the first best and
second best is one of these.

00:21:14.850 --> 00:21:17.780 align:middle line:84%
So if N second best is either
this point or this point,

00:21:17.780 --> 00:21:21.450 align:middle line:84%
the equilibrium number is
somewhere this point or higher,

00:21:21.450 --> 00:21:23.215 align:middle line:84%
we're always getting
too many firms in.

00:21:23.215 --> 00:21:27.830 align:middle line:90%


00:21:27.830 --> 00:21:30.590 align:middle line:90%
Is that OK?

00:21:30.590 --> 00:21:35.710 align:middle line:84%
I have on the next page easier,
maybe simpler intuition.

00:21:35.710 --> 00:21:39.850 align:middle line:84%
In a homogeneous good
model, the only benefit

00:21:39.850 --> 00:21:42.490 align:middle line:84%
that anyone gets
from entry is that we

00:21:42.490 --> 00:21:44.270 align:middle line:90%
reduce the deadweight loss.

00:21:44.270 --> 00:21:46.930 align:middle line:90%
We get quantity increases.

00:21:46.930 --> 00:21:54.220 align:middle line:84%
So what are the total gross
surplus, total consumer and firm

00:21:54.220 --> 00:21:55.970 align:middle line:90%
benefits from the extra entry?

00:21:55.970 --> 00:21:57.910 align:middle line:84%
It's just the
extra quantity that

00:21:57.910 --> 00:22:00.130 align:middle line:84%
gets produced times
p star minus c,

00:22:00.130 --> 00:22:01.930 align:middle line:84%
where p star is
like the equilibrium

00:22:01.930 --> 00:22:04.770 align:middle line:84%
profit you get at the
equilibrium number of firms.

00:22:04.770 --> 00:22:08.380 align:middle line:84%
So when we let in more firms,
the welfare benefit is just,

00:22:08.380 --> 00:22:11.160 align:middle line:84%
how much did quantity go
up times p star minus c?

00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:15.390 align:middle line:90%


00:22:15.390 --> 00:22:18.010 align:middle line:90%
What does the entering firm get?

00:22:18.010 --> 00:22:21.850 align:middle line:84%
The entering firm gets much more
than this because it's not--

00:22:21.850 --> 00:22:24.270 align:middle line:84%
the entering firm is not
just selling the amount

00:22:24.270 --> 00:22:26.580 align:middle line:90%
by which quantity has gone up.

00:22:26.580 --> 00:22:29.450 align:middle line:84%
The entering firm is
getting that quantity--

00:22:29.450 --> 00:22:32.520 align:middle line:84%
it's getting the extra
quantity, but it's also

00:22:32.520 --> 00:22:35.100 align:middle line:84%
stealing some quantity away
from all the other firms that

00:22:35.100 --> 00:22:36.660 align:middle line:90%
were there before.

00:22:36.660 --> 00:22:39.720 align:middle line:84%
And so people call that the
business stealing effect,

00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:41.730 align:middle line:84%
that the amount
by which quantity

00:22:41.730 --> 00:22:43.560 align:middle line:84%
goes up, which is
the welfare gain,

00:22:43.560 --> 00:22:46.680 align:middle line:84%
is much smaller than the
amount by which sales go up

00:22:46.680 --> 00:22:48.740 align:middle line:90%
for the last firm that comes in.

00:22:48.740 --> 00:22:51.960 align:middle line:90%
So the last firm's profits--

00:22:51.960 --> 00:22:54.600 align:middle line:84%
the social cost
of it coming in is

00:22:54.600 --> 00:22:59.610 align:middle line:84%
K. The social benefit of it
coming in is like delta q times

00:22:59.610 --> 00:23:00.840 align:middle line:90%
p minus c.

00:23:00.840 --> 00:23:03.697 align:middle line:84%
But its profits are much bigger
than-- its variable profits are

00:23:03.697 --> 00:23:05.280 align:middle line:84%
much bigger than
delta q times p minus

00:23:05.280 --> 00:23:07.197 align:middle line:84%
c because it's getting
all the profits and all

00:23:07.197 --> 00:23:09.870 align:middle line:84%
the other units it's taking
away from other firms instead.

00:23:09.870 --> 00:23:12.010 align:middle line:84%
And so that's the
business stealing effect.

00:23:12.010 --> 00:23:15.900 align:middle line:84%
And in this
homogeneous good model,

00:23:15.900 --> 00:23:17.650 align:middle line:84%
that business stealing
effect is positive.

00:23:17.650 --> 00:23:19.025 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, you
get too much entry

00:23:19.025 --> 00:23:21.245 align:middle line:84%
other than this possible
rounding error question.

00:23:21.245 --> 00:23:24.070 align:middle line:90%


00:23:24.070 --> 00:23:26.500 align:middle line:84%
As I said, it's true
up to a rounding error,

00:23:26.500 --> 00:23:28.240 align:middle line:84%
but if you do
numerical examples,

00:23:28.240 --> 00:23:30.430 align:middle line:84%
you find that you often
get many, many more

00:23:30.430 --> 00:23:34.780 align:middle line:90%
firms than you'd want socially.

00:23:34.780 --> 00:23:38.650 align:middle line:84%
And the reason is that it's
not just some of the demand

00:23:38.650 --> 00:23:40.083 align:middle line:90%
is business stealing.

00:23:40.083 --> 00:23:41.750 align:middle line:84%
Most of the demand
is business stealing.

00:23:41.750 --> 00:23:45.090 align:middle line:84%
So in this Cournot example,
D of p is 1 minus p.

00:23:45.090 --> 00:23:48.940 align:middle line:90%
q star of N is 1 over N plus 1.

00:23:48.940 --> 00:23:53.530 align:middle line:84%
So what do you get from
adding an Nth firm?

00:23:53.530 --> 00:23:56.560 align:middle line:84%
When you have N firms production
goes up to N over plus 1

00:23:56.560 --> 00:23:59.840 align:middle line:84%
where it used to be
N minus 1 over N,

00:23:59.840 --> 00:24:04.285 align:middle line:84%
so that difference is 1
over N times N plus 1.

00:24:04.285 --> 00:24:07.470 align:middle line:90%


00:24:07.470 --> 00:24:10.800 align:middle line:84%
The Nth firm is producing
1 over N plus 1.

00:24:10.800 --> 00:24:16.260 align:middle line:84%
So 1 over N fraction of its
output is actual added output.

00:24:16.260 --> 00:24:21.220 align:middle line:84%
N minus 1 over N of its output
is business stealing output.

00:24:21.220 --> 00:24:24.660 align:middle line:84%
And so in some sense, the
incentive that it has to enter

00:24:24.660 --> 00:24:31.350 align:middle line:84%
is N times too large because
it's treating 1 over N plus 1

00:24:31.350 --> 00:24:34.500 align:middle line:84%
as the benefit it's getting,
whereas 1 over N times that

00:24:34.500 --> 00:24:37.770 align:middle line:84%
is actually the social
benefit that it's producing.

00:24:37.770 --> 00:24:43.865 align:middle line:84%
And so because private benefit
is N times the social benefit--

00:24:43.865 --> 00:24:45.990 align:middle line:84%
when private benefit is N
times the social benefit,

00:24:45.990 --> 00:24:48.157 align:middle line:84%
you can get way, way too
many firms into the market.

00:24:48.157 --> 00:24:51.650 align:middle line:90%


00:24:51.650 --> 00:24:53.540 align:middle line:90%
OK, so now let me think of it--

00:24:53.540 --> 00:24:56.410 align:middle line:84%
that was entry when you
have homogeneous goods.

00:24:56.410 --> 00:24:58.980 align:middle line:84%
What happens when you get
away from homogeneous goods?

00:24:58.980 --> 00:25:00.920 align:middle line:84%
When you get away from
homogeneous goods,

00:25:00.920 --> 00:25:04.380 align:middle line:84%
entry can be too
high or too low.

00:25:04.380 --> 00:25:05.430 align:middle line:90%
And why is that?

00:25:05.430 --> 00:25:07.750 align:middle line:90%
Well, the general setup--

00:25:07.750 --> 00:25:09.657 align:middle line:84%
welfare when you
have N firms, it's

00:25:09.657 --> 00:25:11.740 align:middle line:84%
going to be the sum of all
the firms in the market

00:25:11.740 --> 00:25:16.030 align:middle line:84%
of their variable profits
plus the total consumer

00:25:16.030 --> 00:25:18.375 align:middle line:84%
surplus in the market
minus the fixed costs.

00:25:18.375 --> 00:25:20.890 align:middle line:90%


00:25:20.890 --> 00:25:26.380 align:middle line:84%
If I add an N plus first
firm, I get the sum over N

00:25:26.380 --> 00:25:29.590 align:middle line:84%
plus 1 firms instead of N
firms of the variable profits.

00:25:29.590 --> 00:25:33.000 align:middle line:84%
I now get consumer surplus
when you have N plus 1 firms,

00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:36.090 align:middle line:90%
but I pay higher fixed costs.

00:25:36.090 --> 00:25:40.560 align:middle line:84%
So what's the change in
welfare from the last entrant?

00:25:40.560 --> 00:25:42.570 align:middle line:84%
First, let me group
together the firm

00:25:42.570 --> 00:25:46.440 align:middle line:84%
that's here that wasn't in
this sum, the firm N plus 1.

00:25:46.440 --> 00:25:48.870 align:middle line:84%
I get the variable
profit of firm N

00:25:48.870 --> 00:25:52.050 align:middle line:90%
plus 1 minus its fixed cost.

00:25:52.050 --> 00:25:54.450 align:middle line:90%
That's a difference here.

00:25:54.450 --> 00:25:56.650 align:middle line:90%
And then I get two other terms.

00:25:56.650 --> 00:26:01.200 align:middle line:84%
The first is subtracting
the corresponding i's here,

00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:05.280 align:middle line:84%
I get sum i equals 1 to N
variable profits of firm i

00:26:05.280 --> 00:26:07.860 align:middle line:90%
with N plus 1 versus N firms.

00:26:07.860 --> 00:26:10.200 align:middle line:84%
And then I get the
consumer surplus with N

00:26:10.200 --> 00:26:11.525 align:middle line:90%
plus 1 minus N firms.

00:26:11.525 --> 00:26:14.860 align:middle line:90%


00:26:14.860 --> 00:26:17.860 align:middle line:84%
So when I wrote here "entry
is socially optimal,"

00:26:17.860 --> 00:26:20.680 align:middle line:84%
I probably should have said
entry of firm N plus 1 is

00:26:20.680 --> 00:26:24.890 align:middle line:84%
socially optimal if this
delta W is positive.

00:26:24.890 --> 00:26:28.550 align:middle line:90%


00:26:28.550 --> 00:26:30.440 align:middle line:90%
Why might free entry--

00:26:30.440 --> 00:26:35.060 align:middle line:84%
so what we want is if this is
positive, we want firm N plus 1

00:26:35.060 --> 00:26:36.060 align:middle line:90%
to come in.

00:26:36.060 --> 00:26:38.900 align:middle line:84%
And if firm-- if
delta W was negative,

00:26:38.900 --> 00:26:42.350 align:middle line:84%
we don't want firm
N plus 1 to come in.

00:26:42.350 --> 00:26:46.170 align:middle line:84%
So first of all, why
might entry be excessive?

00:26:46.170 --> 00:26:49.250 align:middle line:84%
So entry would be excessive
if this was positive

00:26:49.250 --> 00:26:50.930 align:middle line:90%
but this was negative.

00:26:50.930 --> 00:26:53.550 align:middle line:84%
Why could this be positive
and this be negative?

00:26:53.550 --> 00:26:56.270 align:middle line:84%
Well, if this is
positive but small

00:26:56.270 --> 00:27:00.690 align:middle line:84%
to make a firm want
to come in but this

00:27:00.690 --> 00:27:02.610 align:middle line:90%
is a big negative number--

00:27:02.610 --> 00:27:04.500 align:middle line:84%
and why could this be
a big negative number?

00:27:04.500 --> 00:27:06.960 align:middle line:84%
This is how every
other firm's profits

00:27:06.960 --> 00:27:09.020 align:middle line:90%
are affected by me coming in.

00:27:09.020 --> 00:27:12.210 align:middle line:84%
If I drive down prices
and I take quantity away

00:27:12.210 --> 00:27:16.410 align:middle line:84%
from the other firms, they
suffer both because the profits

00:27:16.410 --> 00:27:19.090 align:middle line:84%
they earn on the units
they still sell go down

00:27:19.090 --> 00:27:21.360 align:middle line:84%
and because they
don't sell some units

00:27:21.360 --> 00:27:23.480 align:middle line:84%
and they don't earn
p star minus c.

00:27:23.480 --> 00:27:25.470 align:middle line:84%
So this is what we
call-- again, this is

00:27:25.470 --> 00:27:26.700 align:middle line:90%
the business stealing effect.

00:27:26.700 --> 00:27:29.680 align:middle line:84%
If this business stealing
number is always negative--

00:27:29.680 --> 00:27:31.740 align:middle line:84%
if it's a big negative
number and this

00:27:31.740 --> 00:27:35.220 align:middle line:84%
is a small positive number, we
can get the entrant coming in,

00:27:35.220 --> 00:27:37.510 align:middle line:84%
even though this business
stealing term is negative.

00:27:37.510 --> 00:27:40.040 align:middle line:90%


00:27:40.040 --> 00:27:43.130 align:middle line:84%
But then the second
effect is that we

00:27:43.130 --> 00:27:48.750 align:middle line:84%
could have firm N doesn't come
in because this is negative.

00:27:48.750 --> 00:27:52.350 align:middle line:84%
And this is negative
but delta W is positive

00:27:52.350 --> 00:27:58.660 align:middle line:84%
because there's a big
increase in consumer surplus.

00:27:58.660 --> 00:28:01.220 align:middle line:84%
And I showed the
homogeneous good model

00:28:01.220 --> 00:28:05.310 align:middle line:84%
this doesn't happen except with
that rounding error effect.

00:28:05.310 --> 00:28:08.410 align:middle line:84%
But once you get to
heterogeneous goods,

00:28:08.410 --> 00:28:10.960 align:middle line:84%
it could be that when you
get more goods in the market,

00:28:10.960 --> 00:28:13.510 align:middle line:84%
consumer surplus goes
up by more than prices

00:28:13.510 --> 00:28:16.150 align:middle line:84%
go down because consumers
get a good that's

00:28:16.150 --> 00:28:18.160 align:middle line:90%
better matched to their tastes.

00:28:18.160 --> 00:28:21.230 align:middle line:84%
And because the new good is
better matched to their tastes,

00:28:21.230 --> 00:28:23.590 align:middle line:84%
there's a big increase
in consumer surplus

00:28:23.590 --> 00:28:26.320 align:middle line:84%
that isn't just the
loss and profits.

00:28:26.320 --> 00:28:28.120 align:middle line:84%
And so if the increase
in consumer surplus

00:28:28.120 --> 00:28:31.150 align:middle line:84%
is coming from other
channels like match quality

00:28:31.150 --> 00:28:34.000 align:middle line:84%
rather than just from
prices going down,

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:38.840 align:middle line:84%
then this term can outweigh
the business stealing effect

00:28:38.840 --> 00:28:42.730 align:middle line:84%
and you could have too few firms
in a market because firms--

00:28:42.730 --> 00:28:44.300 align:middle line:90%
firms don't like--

00:28:44.300 --> 00:28:47.020 align:middle line:84%
I mean, firms not
internalizing consumer surplus

00:28:47.020 --> 00:28:49.510 align:middle line:84%
is why you get too few
firms in the Bertrand model.

00:28:49.510 --> 00:28:52.960 align:middle line:84%
In the Bertrand model, firms
don't realize I come in, yes, I

00:28:52.960 --> 00:28:55.090 align:middle line:84%
lose some money, but
all those consumers

00:28:55.090 --> 00:28:59.840 align:middle line:84%
are way better off because
the price drops so much.

00:28:59.840 --> 00:29:02.490 align:middle line:84%
But in another model, it's
the consumers don't realize--

00:29:02.490 --> 00:29:04.468 align:middle line:84%
the firms don't realize
that, like, I come in,

00:29:04.468 --> 00:29:06.260 align:middle line:84%
I don't make that much
money, but consumers

00:29:06.260 --> 00:29:10.780 align:middle line:84%
are so happy to have my product
that social welfare is better.

00:29:10.780 --> 00:29:16.360 align:middle line:90%


00:29:16.360 --> 00:29:18.250 align:middle line:90%
OK, any questions?

00:29:18.250 --> 00:29:20.800 align:middle line:84%
So in a general model, the
most general conclusion

00:29:20.800 --> 00:29:23.150 align:middle line:84%
is that entry can be too
high or too low socially.

00:29:23.150 --> 00:29:26.430 align:middle line:84%
And it depends on which of
these effects is more important,

00:29:26.430 --> 00:29:30.070 align:middle line:84%
the social welfare effect or
the business stealing effect.

00:29:30.070 --> 00:29:34.490 align:middle line:84%
Prices going down alone
is not enough to do it.

00:29:34.490 --> 00:29:36.760 align:middle line:84%
You've got to have other
channels of the consumer

00:29:36.760 --> 00:29:37.830 align:middle line:90%
surplus going up.

00:29:37.830 --> 00:29:44.630 align:middle line:90%


00:29:44.630 --> 00:29:47.870 align:middle line:84%
I just figured I'd put in one
slide of what happens if I

00:29:47.870 --> 00:29:49.760 align:middle line:90%
actually compute these things.

00:29:49.760 --> 00:29:53.670 align:middle line:84%
I would say that if you write
down the most obvious models

00:29:53.670 --> 00:29:56.460 align:middle line:84%
you're going to write down to
try to compute these things.

00:29:56.460 --> 00:30:00.420 align:middle line:84%
While this consumer surplus
effect means that welfare can--

00:30:00.420 --> 00:30:04.385 align:middle line:84%
we can get too few firms,
most models you write down,

00:30:04.385 --> 00:30:06.510 align:middle line:84%
if you're not thinking
about writing down something

00:30:06.510 --> 00:30:07.800 align:middle line:84%
to get it to
happen, you're going

00:30:07.800 --> 00:30:09.258 align:middle line:84%
to also conclude
that there's still

00:30:09.258 --> 00:30:10.960 align:middle line:90%
way too many firms in markets.

00:30:10.960 --> 00:30:13.240 align:middle line:90%
So I do an example here.

00:30:13.240 --> 00:30:16.320 align:middle line:84%
So consider a
Hotelling-like model, where

00:30:16.320 --> 00:30:18.570 align:middle line:90%
you have a mass m of consumers.

00:30:18.570 --> 00:30:21.210 align:middle line:90%
And I'll write over here.

00:30:21.210 --> 00:30:25.540 align:middle line:84%
So let's think about
a Hotelling circle.

00:30:25.540 --> 00:30:27.030 align:middle line:90%
So this is, ah--

00:30:27.030 --> 00:30:31.080 align:middle line:84%
ice cream stands locating around
like a Hawaiian island that

00:30:31.080 --> 00:30:33.660 align:middle line:84%
has just beaches around
the entire perimeter.

00:30:33.660 --> 00:30:35.253 align:middle line:90%
And so if you get N firms--

00:30:35.253 --> 00:30:38.950 align:middle line:90%


00:30:38.950 --> 00:30:41.040 align:middle line:84%
suppose you get N firms;
they locate themselves

00:30:41.040 --> 00:30:42.245 align:middle line:90%
evenly around the circle.

00:30:42.245 --> 00:30:47.880 align:middle line:90%


00:30:47.880 --> 00:30:50.880 align:middle line:90%
In the c plus t model--

00:30:50.880 --> 00:30:55.560 align:middle line:84%
if you have N firms, the
transportation cost distance

00:30:55.560 --> 00:30:57.780 align:middle line:84%
between here and
here is only going

00:30:57.780 --> 00:30:59.970 align:middle line:84%
to be t over N when
you have N firms.

00:30:59.970 --> 00:31:03.690 align:middle line:84%
So if it's t to walk the
whole perimeter of the island,

00:31:03.690 --> 00:31:07.360 align:middle line:84%
it's t over N when you put N
firms around the perimeter.

00:31:07.360 --> 00:31:11.640 align:middle line:84%
So if you get N firms
that are arranged evenly,

00:31:11.640 --> 00:31:13.650 align:middle line:84%
what you basically
get is each firm here

00:31:13.650 --> 00:31:15.570 align:middle line:84%
is just competing with
the firm on its right

00:31:15.570 --> 00:31:17.250 align:middle line:90%
and the firm on its left.

00:31:17.250 --> 00:31:20.430 align:middle line:84%
I didn't draw those so
evenly, but let's assume

00:31:20.430 --> 00:31:23.160 align:middle line:84%
this distance is t over N,
this distance is t over N.

00:31:23.160 --> 00:31:25.650 align:middle line:84%
So it's just like I'm playing
two simultaneous Hotelling

00:31:25.650 --> 00:31:28.590 align:middle line:84%
games, one against this
firm, one against this firm.

00:31:28.590 --> 00:31:35.740 align:middle line:84%
So the equilibrium becomes p
star equals c plus t over N.

00:31:35.740 --> 00:31:38.020 align:middle line:90%
What's the variable profit?

00:31:38.020 --> 00:31:41.620 align:middle line:84%
If there are m
consumers on the beach,

00:31:41.620 --> 00:31:44.440 align:middle line:84%
I get a fraction 1 over
N of them in equilibrium,

00:31:44.440 --> 00:31:56.500 align:middle line:84%
and my markup is t over N. So my
profits are mt over N squared.

00:31:56.500 --> 00:31:58.990 align:middle line:84%
I set that equal
to K, and then it

00:31:58.990 --> 00:32:03.220 align:middle line:84%
just becomes N equals square
root of m times t over K.

00:32:03.220 --> 00:32:06.790 align:middle line:84%
So again, as I said,
this is, again,

00:32:06.790 --> 00:32:12.180 align:middle line:84%
a larger firms theorem that
says as the market size goes up,

00:32:12.180 --> 00:32:13.930 align:middle line:84%
the number of firms
goes up in this model,

00:32:13.930 --> 00:32:16.446 align:middle line:84%
also like the square root
of the number of customers.

00:32:16.446 --> 00:32:20.180 align:middle line:90%


00:32:20.180 --> 00:32:22.670 align:middle line:90%
What's the social welfare?

00:32:22.670 --> 00:32:24.066 align:middle line:90%
There are m consumers.

00:32:24.066 --> 00:32:29.165 align:middle line:90%


00:32:29.165 --> 00:32:31.290 align:middle line:84%
So suppose we're in this
model and these things are

00:32:31.290 --> 00:32:38.550 align:middle line:84%
both t over N. Social welfare
is just total gross surplus

00:32:38.550 --> 00:32:42.330 align:middle line:84%
received minus
costs of production

00:32:42.330 --> 00:32:46.980 align:middle line:84%
minus average mismatch
disutility or transportation

00:32:46.980 --> 00:32:50.730 align:middle line:90%
cost disutility.

00:32:50.730 --> 00:32:55.470 align:middle line:84%
This guy here sells to customers
who are within distance t over

00:32:55.470 --> 00:33:03.990 align:middle line:84%
2N to the left and customers
within t over 2N to the right.

00:33:03.990 --> 00:33:07.030 align:middle line:84%
So he's selling to customers who
are-- t over N is this distance.

00:33:07.030 --> 00:33:09.960 align:middle line:84%
So everybody equidistant
between him and the next firm

00:33:09.960 --> 00:33:11.490 align:middle line:90%
over buys from him.

00:33:11.490 --> 00:33:15.120 align:middle line:84%
If you sell to customers whose
distance ranges from 0 to t over

00:33:15.120 --> 00:33:19.950 align:middle line:84%
2N, the average distance that
people have to travel is t over

00:33:19.950 --> 00:33:21.030 align:middle line:90%
4N.

00:33:21.030 --> 00:33:24.180 align:middle line:84%
So this is the extra welfare
increase that I mentioned,

00:33:24.180 --> 00:33:28.570 align:middle line:84%
that as N increases, the
distance traveled goes down.

00:33:28.570 --> 00:33:30.690 align:middle line:84%
That causes a consumer
surplus increase

00:33:30.690 --> 00:33:32.070 align:middle line:84%
that's separate
from the one that

00:33:32.070 --> 00:33:35.400 align:middle line:90%
comes from the prices dropping.

00:33:35.400 --> 00:33:39.630 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so in this model, total
social welfare is m times v

00:33:39.630 --> 00:33:45.820 align:middle line:84%
minus m times c minus m
times t over 4N minus NK.

00:33:45.820 --> 00:33:49.420 align:middle line:84%
Notice that m times v minus c
is completely independent of N.

00:33:49.420 --> 00:33:52.390 align:middle line:84%
And the Hotelling model,
assuming v is large,

00:33:52.390 --> 00:33:54.490 align:middle line:90%
everybody buys all the time.

00:33:54.490 --> 00:33:57.640 align:middle line:90%
So what we get is--

00:33:57.640 --> 00:34:00.100 align:middle line:84%
differentiating this
with respect to N,

00:34:00.100 --> 00:34:03.670 align:middle line:84%
derivative of this with respect
to N is mt over 4 times 1

00:34:03.670 --> 00:34:05.320 align:middle line:90%
over N squared.

00:34:05.320 --> 00:34:09.280 align:middle line:84%
Derivative of this with
respect to N is just K.

00:34:09.280 --> 00:34:13.030 align:middle line:84%
And so what I get is that m over
4 times 1 over N squared minus

00:34:13.030 --> 00:34:16.330 align:middle line:84%
K, if I add N second
best equals 0,

00:34:16.330 --> 00:34:20.020 align:middle line:84%
N second best is square
root of mt over 4K,

00:34:20.020 --> 00:34:25.659 align:middle line:90%
which is exactly half of N star.

00:34:25.659 --> 00:34:27.790 align:middle line:84%
So this is saying, in some
sense, in equilibrium,

00:34:27.790 --> 00:34:32.900 align:middle line:84%
you get exactly twice as
many firms as you would like.

00:34:32.900 --> 00:34:34.130 align:middle line:90%
And this is just--

00:34:34.130 --> 00:34:35.975 align:middle line:84%
it's another case
that in this model--

00:34:35.975 --> 00:34:38.719 align:middle line:90%


00:34:38.719 --> 00:34:40.760 align:middle line:84%
so this model has
the welfare benefit

00:34:40.760 --> 00:34:43.040 align:middle line:84%
of people getting
better matches.

00:34:43.040 --> 00:34:46.260 align:middle line:84%
This model has no market
expansion effect whatsoever.

00:34:46.260 --> 00:34:49.850 align:middle line:84%
When you put in an Nth firm,
it's 100% business stealing.

00:34:49.850 --> 00:34:53.060 align:middle line:84%
And so it turns out that
because the market expansion is

00:34:53.060 --> 00:34:56.870 align:middle line:84%
100% business stealing, so
business stealing is a big deal,

00:34:56.870 --> 00:35:00.410 align:middle line:84%
this extra benefit that you
get from extra match quality

00:35:00.410 --> 00:35:03.650 align:middle line:84%
isn't nearly large enough to
overcome the business stealing

00:35:03.650 --> 00:35:06.860 align:middle line:84%
negative, and you're
getting twice as many firms.

00:35:06.860 --> 00:35:09.980 align:middle line:84%
And I think, in
some sense, I view

00:35:09.980 --> 00:35:12.280 align:middle line:84%
this as an important
cautionary tale.

00:35:12.280 --> 00:35:14.702 align:middle line:84%
You're going to write down
models with logit demand

00:35:14.702 --> 00:35:16.660 align:middle line:84%
and estimate how many
firms you're going to get

00:35:16.660 --> 00:35:19.750 align:middle line:84%
and how many firms you think
are optimal or whatever.

00:35:19.750 --> 00:35:22.162 align:middle line:84%
A lot of models like
this are set up-- again,

00:35:22.162 --> 00:35:23.620 align:middle line:84%
I could have done
this model, and I

00:35:23.620 --> 00:35:26.230 align:middle line:84%
could have said I'm
estimating demand

00:35:26.230 --> 00:35:29.350 align:middle line:90%
elasticities by estimating t.

00:35:29.350 --> 00:35:33.250 align:middle line:84%
But whatever value of t I
estimated when I estimated

00:35:33.250 --> 00:35:34.865 align:middle line:90%
this model, my model--

00:35:34.865 --> 00:35:36.490 align:middle line:84%
I do my counterfactual
and say how many

00:35:36.490 --> 00:35:38.365 align:middle line:84%
firms would be socially
optimal, and socially

00:35:38.365 --> 00:35:41.140 align:middle line:84%
optimal would be exactly half
the number that are observed.

00:35:41.140 --> 00:35:44.030 align:middle line:84%
So it's just important
to be aware of.

00:35:44.030 --> 00:35:48.065 align:middle line:84%
Do models have built-in answers
as to whether the consumer

00:35:48.065 --> 00:35:50.440 align:middle line:84%
surplus effect is going to
outweigh the business stealing

00:35:50.440 --> 00:35:51.230 align:middle line:90%
effect or not?

00:35:51.230 --> 00:35:52.730 align:middle line:84%
And this one, it's
totally built in.

00:35:52.730 --> 00:35:55.510 align:middle line:84%
And the answer is
it's a factor of 2.

00:35:55.510 --> 00:35:58.420 align:middle line:90%
But I think it's generally true.

00:35:58.420 --> 00:36:03.087 align:middle line:84%
Most models you write down,
you are going to get a factor--

00:36:03.087 --> 00:36:05.420 align:middle line:84%
you are going to find that
there are way too many firms.

00:36:05.420 --> 00:36:07.240 align:middle line:90%
And I don't know if I--

00:36:07.240 --> 00:36:08.860 align:middle line:90%
maybe I believe that.

00:36:08.860 --> 00:36:09.813 align:middle line:90%
How would you get--

00:36:09.813 --> 00:36:13.450 align:middle line:90%


00:36:13.450 --> 00:36:19.650 align:middle line:84%
how would you get bigger
social welfare benefits?

00:36:19.650 --> 00:36:22.690 align:middle line:84%
There's two ways you would get
bigger social welfare benefits.

00:36:22.690 --> 00:36:25.950 align:middle line:84%
The first is-- in this
model, people's utility

00:36:25.950 --> 00:36:30.150 align:middle line:84%
for getting a product that's not
ideal just goes down linearly.

00:36:30.150 --> 00:36:34.350 align:middle line:84%
If you imagine people's
utility looked more like this,

00:36:34.350 --> 00:36:38.280 align:middle line:84%
so there are people
who really, really like

00:36:38.280 --> 00:36:42.630 align:middle line:84%
having a product that's
very close to their--

00:36:42.630 --> 00:36:44.440 align:middle line:90%
very close to their ideal point.

00:36:44.440 --> 00:36:47.160 align:middle line:84%
So when you add an extra and
have three ideal points instead

00:36:47.160 --> 00:36:49.950 align:middle line:84%
of two, you're giving
some people lots and lots

00:36:49.950 --> 00:36:51.340 align:middle line:90%
of extra utility.

00:36:51.340 --> 00:36:54.840 align:middle line:84%
So having something
like this, people

00:36:54.840 --> 00:36:57.120 align:middle line:84%
who like things very close
to their ideal point,

00:36:57.120 --> 00:37:03.240 align:middle line:84%
would be one way to get
entry to be not too high.

00:37:03.240 --> 00:37:05.028 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, you can
only go so high here

00:37:05.028 --> 00:37:07.320 align:middle line:84%
before the firm is going to
give up on serving everyone

00:37:07.320 --> 00:37:09.487 align:middle line:84%
and just serve the people
who like its product a lot

00:37:09.487 --> 00:37:10.862 align:middle line:90%
at a really high price.

00:37:10.862 --> 00:37:13.320 align:middle line:84%
So that's going to be kind of
like those information design

00:37:13.320 --> 00:37:16.010 align:middle line:84%
things where you can only
make the curve go up so much

00:37:16.010 --> 00:37:18.220 align:middle line:84%
and have the firm still
sell to everybody.

00:37:18.220 --> 00:37:22.300 align:middle line:84%
And then the other effect is
here, again, 100% of your demand

00:37:22.300 --> 00:37:23.300 align:middle line:90%
is business stealing.

00:37:23.300 --> 00:37:25.600 align:middle line:84%
If you think of an infinite
dimensional space, where

00:37:25.600 --> 00:37:28.420 align:middle line:84%
most people are not
buying any product at all,

00:37:28.420 --> 00:37:32.620 align:middle line:84%
so every new firm that comes in
is mostly serving new customers.

00:37:32.620 --> 00:37:36.280 align:middle line:84%
So if you can make the
business stealing effect small

00:37:36.280 --> 00:37:39.408 align:middle line:84%
and make this consumer
surplus large for people

00:37:39.408 --> 00:37:41.200 align:middle line:84%
who get close matches,
those are the things

00:37:41.200 --> 00:37:45.390 align:middle line:84%
that are going to make entry
be insufficient in practice.

00:37:45.390 --> 00:37:49.000 align:middle line:84%
And I think if you want
to do an empirical study,

00:37:49.000 --> 00:37:50.590 align:middle line:84%
you'd really want
to think about,

00:37:50.590 --> 00:37:53.170 align:middle line:84%
do I have free
parameters to capture

00:37:53.170 --> 00:37:54.560 align:middle line:90%
what this surplus looks like?

00:37:54.560 --> 00:37:56.750 align:middle line:84%
And do I have a free
parameter to capture

00:37:56.750 --> 00:37:59.590 align:middle line:84%
how much of the
people are being added

00:37:59.590 --> 00:38:03.370 align:middle line:84%
to the model versus how many
people are just business stolen

00:38:03.370 --> 00:38:04.125 align:middle line:90%
from another firm?

00:38:04.125 --> 00:38:08.040 align:middle line:90%


00:38:08.040 --> 00:38:10.900 align:middle line:84%
For instance, I read that nested
logit has that extra parameter

00:38:10.900 --> 00:38:12.900 align:middle line:84%
that substitution
to the outside good

00:38:12.900 --> 00:38:15.660 align:middle line:84%
is different from substitution
to the inside goods.

00:38:15.660 --> 00:38:18.150 align:middle line:84%
And a pure logit
with no outside good

00:38:18.150 --> 00:38:19.895 align:middle line:90%
is 100% business stealing model.

00:38:19.895 --> 00:38:22.420 align:middle line:90%


00:38:22.420 --> 00:38:23.050 align:middle line:90%
Questions?

00:38:23.050 --> 00:38:25.640 align:middle line:90%


00:38:25.640 --> 00:38:29.390 align:middle line:84%
Entry with vertical
differentiation.

00:38:29.390 --> 00:38:32.660 align:middle line:84%
OK, so let's go back
again and review

00:38:32.660 --> 00:38:35.540 align:middle line:84%
our classic vertical
differentiation model.

00:38:35.540 --> 00:38:38.960 align:middle line:84%
You have consumers that have
these vertical types theta.

00:38:38.960 --> 00:38:41.330 align:middle line:84%
They have unit demands
where your utility

00:38:41.330 --> 00:38:45.560 align:middle line:84%
is theta times the quality
of the good minus p.

00:38:45.560 --> 00:38:49.280 align:middle line:84%
Let's assume that firms
have fixed entry costs K.

00:38:49.280 --> 00:38:51.950 align:middle line:84%
And then they have constant
marginal cost c of s,

00:38:51.950 --> 00:38:53.810 align:middle line:84%
where the higher
quality products

00:38:53.810 --> 00:38:56.750 align:middle line:90%
are more expensive to produce.

00:38:56.750 --> 00:38:59.400 align:middle line:90%
So consider a two-stage game.

00:38:59.400 --> 00:39:01.220 align:middle line:84%
The first, the firms
are simultaneously

00:39:01.220 --> 00:39:02.960 align:middle line:90%
choosing whether to enter.

00:39:02.960 --> 00:39:04.940 align:middle line:84%
And if they enter,
they're going to choose

00:39:04.940 --> 00:39:07.850 align:middle line:90%
which quality level to produce.

00:39:07.850 --> 00:39:09.713 align:middle line:84%
And then, once
they've done that,

00:39:09.713 --> 00:39:12.380 align:middle line:84%
they compete in prices according
to the vertical differentiation

00:39:12.380 --> 00:39:14.770 align:middle line:90%
model.

00:39:14.770 --> 00:39:17.620 align:middle line:84%
And Shaked and Sutton
have this classic paper

00:39:17.620 --> 00:39:22.330 align:middle line:84%
that shows that you can get
two different types of outcomes

00:39:22.330 --> 00:39:24.400 align:middle line:90%
in this.

00:39:24.400 --> 00:39:28.485 align:middle line:84%
First one is what they call
the natural oligopoly case.

00:39:28.485 --> 00:39:31.670 align:middle line:90%


00:39:31.670 --> 00:39:40.482 align:middle line:84%
So imagine that if I put
s on this axis and I put--

00:39:40.482 --> 00:39:42.115 align:middle line:90%
what am I going to do?--

00:39:42.115 --> 00:39:45.320 align:middle line:90%


00:39:45.320 --> 00:39:48.770 align:middle line:84%
either the value that they
get from s, theta s minus p,

00:39:48.770 --> 00:39:51.610 align:middle line:84%
on this axis or I put
c of s on this axis.

00:39:51.610 --> 00:39:54.600 align:middle line:90%


00:39:54.600 --> 00:40:03.180 align:middle line:84%
So imagine that-- imagine
that you have relatively

00:40:03.180 --> 00:40:05.610 align:middle line:90%
flat marginal cost curves.

00:40:05.610 --> 00:40:09.360 align:middle line:84%
So imagine that
marginal cost c of s

00:40:09.360 --> 00:40:12.260 align:middle line:84%
looks like this,
that there's just not

00:40:12.260 --> 00:40:15.650 align:middle line:84%
that much cost to producing
high-quality goods.

00:40:15.650 --> 00:40:21.230 align:middle line:84%
And suppose that you
have like theta lower bar

00:40:21.230 --> 00:40:23.570 align:middle line:90%
times s looks like this.

00:40:23.570 --> 00:40:32.230 align:middle line:84%
And theta upper bar s
minus p looks like that.

00:40:32.230 --> 00:40:35.260 align:middle line:84%
So we have a model where
everybody, even the lowest

00:40:35.260 --> 00:40:39.280 align:middle line:84%
possible types, value quality
more than it costs to produce

00:40:39.280 --> 00:40:41.980 align:middle line:90%
the extra units of quality.

00:40:41.980 --> 00:40:47.230 align:middle line:84%
And there's some range
here, s lower bar,

00:40:47.230 --> 00:40:48.850 align:middle line:84%
s upper bar of
the qualities that

00:40:48.850 --> 00:40:50.070 align:middle line:90%
are available to be produced.

00:40:50.070 --> 00:40:52.828 align:middle line:90%


00:40:52.828 --> 00:40:57.390 align:middle line:84%
In a model like this, what they
call the natural oligopoly case,

00:40:57.390 --> 00:40:59.888 align:middle line:90%
everybody should--

00:40:59.888 --> 00:41:02.430 align:middle line:84%
in an efficient market, everyone
should be buying the highest

00:41:02.430 --> 00:41:05.080 align:middle line:90%
possible quality.

00:41:05.080 --> 00:41:09.220 align:middle line:84%
And so if you imagine
what happens--

00:41:09.220 --> 00:41:16.670 align:middle line:84%
suppose you have lots of
firms entering and producing

00:41:16.670 --> 00:41:18.330 align:middle line:90%
different qualities.

00:41:18.330 --> 00:41:22.610 align:middle line:84%
And then what are they going
to set their prices at?

00:41:22.610 --> 00:41:25.190 align:middle line:84%
Well, if you have lots
of firms in the market,

00:41:25.190 --> 00:41:27.860 align:middle line:90%
the prices are going to fall--

00:41:27.860 --> 00:41:30.680 align:middle line:84%
these are prices; I'm
putting dots in here--

00:41:30.680 --> 00:41:33.140 align:middle line:84%
the prices are going to have
to fall towards cost as N goes

00:41:33.140 --> 00:41:35.720 align:middle line:84%
to-- as the number of firms
into this finite product space

00:41:35.720 --> 00:41:37.310 align:middle line:90%
goes to infinity.

00:41:37.310 --> 00:41:40.400 align:middle line:84%
But then if the prices
fall towards cost,

00:41:40.400 --> 00:41:43.720 align:middle line:84%
everyone is going to buy from
the highest quality firm.

00:41:43.720 --> 00:41:47.590 align:middle line:84%
And so you just can't have
prices falling to cost,

00:41:47.590 --> 00:41:51.322 align:middle line:84%
because if prices fall to cost,
all these firms make zero sales,

00:41:51.322 --> 00:41:52.780 align:middle line:84%
and then they earn
negative profits

00:41:52.780 --> 00:41:56.135 align:middle line:90%
after paying their fixed cost K.

00:41:56.135 --> 00:41:58.900 align:middle line:84%
So if you have this case
where everybody should

00:41:58.900 --> 00:42:01.870 align:middle line:84%
buy the same product,
there's only a finite number

00:42:01.870 --> 00:42:02.870 align:middle line:90%
of firms in the market.

00:42:02.870 --> 00:42:06.940 align:middle line:84%
Again, this isn't an N goes
to infinity as K goes to 0.

00:42:06.940 --> 00:42:10.610 align:middle line:84%
As K goes to 0, you still
get a finite number of firms.

00:42:10.610 --> 00:42:13.390 align:middle line:84%
It may be this market can fit
three firms or five or six

00:42:13.390 --> 00:42:15.790 align:middle line:84%
or whatever, but there's
a finite amount of entry

00:42:15.790 --> 00:42:19.810 align:middle line:84%
that can ever occur
if the same product is

00:42:19.810 --> 00:42:21.225 align:middle line:90%
efficient for everyone to buy.

00:42:21.225 --> 00:42:24.490 align:middle line:90%


00:42:24.490 --> 00:42:28.210 align:middle line:84%
And then the second case they
talk about is the specialization

00:42:28.210 --> 00:42:29.440 align:middle line:90%
case.

00:42:29.440 --> 00:42:36.010 align:middle line:84%
The specialization case would be
if you have marginal costs that

00:42:36.010 --> 00:42:37.600 align:middle line:90%
work like this.

00:42:37.600 --> 00:42:43.370 align:middle line:84%
Marginal costs
are initially low,

00:42:43.370 --> 00:42:45.160 align:middle line:84%
but then they
shoot up like that.

00:42:45.160 --> 00:42:49.890 align:middle line:90%


00:42:49.890 --> 00:42:52.710 align:middle line:84%
If the marginal
costs look like this,

00:42:52.710 --> 00:42:55.210 align:middle line:84%
what's efficient for the
theta lower bar types?

00:42:55.210 --> 00:43:00.673 align:middle line:84%
The theta lower bar types
would pick the point--

00:43:00.673 --> 00:43:02.090 align:middle line:84%
what's efficient
for them to do is

00:43:02.090 --> 00:43:05.385 align:middle line:84%
where the marginal cost
of producing extra quality

00:43:05.385 --> 00:43:07.510 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the marginal
benefit that they receive.

00:43:07.510 --> 00:43:10.380 align:middle line:90%
So the theta lower bar types--

00:43:10.380 --> 00:43:16.410 align:middle line:84%
so this would be s star optimal
for the theta lower bar types.

00:43:16.410 --> 00:43:18.150 align:middle line:84%
They want to
produce where-- they

00:43:18.150 --> 00:43:20.970 align:middle line:84%
should be buying the good where
the marginal value of increasing

00:43:20.970 --> 00:43:23.670 align:middle line:84%
quality is exactly equal to
their willingness to pay.

00:43:23.670 --> 00:43:25.572 align:middle line:90%
And the theta upper bar types--

00:43:25.572 --> 00:43:27.155 align:middle line:84%
maybe I should have
made this steeper.

00:43:27.155 --> 00:43:31.200 align:middle line:84%
[CHUCKLES] Let me make theta
upper bar even steeper.

00:43:31.200 --> 00:43:33.300 align:middle line:84%
The theta upper
bar types are going

00:43:33.300 --> 00:43:37.080 align:middle line:84%
to have a point where, again,
they're going to buy up here,

00:43:37.080 --> 00:43:39.240 align:middle line:90%
where it has this greater slope.

00:43:39.240 --> 00:43:42.750 align:middle line:84%
So the s star theta
upper bar, they

00:43:42.750 --> 00:43:45.090 align:middle line:90%
should be buying this product.

00:43:45.090 --> 00:43:48.570 align:middle line:84%
And everybody with
a theta in between

00:43:48.570 --> 00:43:50.650 align:middle line:84%
should be buying
something in between.

00:43:50.650 --> 00:43:51.930 align:middle line:90%
Everybody should be picking--

00:43:51.930 --> 00:43:53.580 align:middle line:84%
the optimal product
for everybody

00:43:53.580 --> 00:43:57.930 align:middle line:84%
is the point on that
curve where it's

00:43:57.930 --> 00:44:00.240 align:middle line:90%
an efficient quality for them.

00:44:00.240 --> 00:44:03.340 align:middle line:84%
So if you have a model like
this where efficient quality--

00:44:03.340 --> 00:44:06.060 align:middle line:90%


00:44:06.060 --> 00:44:08.100 align:middle line:84%
everybody has a different
efficient quality,

00:44:08.100 --> 00:44:09.810 align:middle line:84%
then as N goes to
infinity, you are

00:44:09.810 --> 00:44:11.840 align:middle line:84%
going to get an
infinitely divided model.

00:44:11.840 --> 00:44:14.470 align:middle line:84%
So every product is
efficient for someone.

00:44:14.470 --> 00:44:16.480 align:middle line:84%
So if the number of
customers is large,

00:44:16.480 --> 00:44:17.990 align:middle line:90%
all those products get produced.

00:44:17.990 --> 00:44:20.210 align:middle line:84%
They all sell to 1
over N of the people.

00:44:20.210 --> 00:44:22.510 align:middle line:84%
They sell to the people
whose prices are--

00:44:22.510 --> 00:44:27.130 align:middle line:84%
whose marginal values are
close to their marginal--

00:44:27.130 --> 00:44:31.100 align:middle line:84%
whose optimal qualities
are close to their quality

00:44:31.100 --> 00:44:34.010 align:middle line:84%
and prices do fall
towards marginal cost.

00:44:34.010 --> 00:44:36.250 align:middle line:84%
But even though prices
fall to marginal cost,

00:44:36.250 --> 00:44:40.750 align:middle line:84%
everybody still buys it
from a different firm.

00:44:40.750 --> 00:44:44.290 align:middle line:84%
So what happens with vertical
differentiation is sometimes

00:44:44.290 --> 00:44:46.930 align:middle line:84%
you can't get a lot of
firms, but sometimes you

00:44:46.930 --> 00:44:48.080 align:middle line:90%
get the specialization.

00:44:48.080 --> 00:44:51.160 align:middle line:84%
And it all depends on whether
different products are

00:44:51.160 --> 00:44:52.380 align:middle line:90%
optimal for different people.

00:44:52.380 --> 00:44:57.140 align:middle line:90%


00:44:57.140 --> 00:44:58.060 align:middle line:90%
Questions?

00:44:58.060 --> 00:45:03.540 align:middle line:90%


00:45:03.540 --> 00:45:08.040 align:middle line:84%
OK, so next thing-- next
set of papers I want--

00:45:08.040 --> 00:45:09.930 align:middle line:84%
next paper I want
to discuss is--

00:45:09.930 --> 00:45:12.450 align:middle line:90%
this paper is motivated by--

00:45:12.450 --> 00:45:17.700 align:middle line:84%
there's a large literature
on firm dynamics

00:45:17.700 --> 00:45:21.480 align:middle line:84%
and entry and exit that's
generated by people

00:45:21.480 --> 00:45:23.970 align:middle line:90%
looking at census data.

00:45:23.970 --> 00:45:26.468 align:middle line:90%
So I don't know if I mentioned--

00:45:26.468 --> 00:45:28.510 align:middle line:84%
I think I mentioned this
earlier in the semester.

00:45:28.510 --> 00:45:32.930 align:middle line:84%
So there's this great
resource for IO economists

00:45:32.930 --> 00:45:37.710 align:middle line:84%
which is called the
Census of Manufacturers.

00:45:37.710 --> 00:45:51.980 align:middle line:84%
So the Census of Manufacturers
is conducted by the US Census

00:45:51.980 --> 00:45:55.520 align:middle line:84%
Bureau every five
years, all the years

00:45:55.520 --> 00:45:58.310 align:middle line:90%
that end in twos and sevens.

00:45:58.310 --> 00:46:00.920 align:middle line:84%
Census of Manufacturers,
it's a complete census

00:46:00.920 --> 00:46:05.240 align:middle line:84%
of every manufacturing
firm in the United States.

00:46:05.240 --> 00:46:06.950 align:middle line:84%
In addition, the
census does something

00:46:06.950 --> 00:46:08.888 align:middle line:84%
called the Annual
Survey of Manufacturers.

00:46:08.888 --> 00:46:10.430 align:middle line:84%
It takes a subset
of this and samples

00:46:10.430 --> 00:46:14.480 align:middle line:84%
them to produce annual data
instead of every-five-year data.

00:46:14.480 --> 00:46:18.610 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, they
send out census forms

00:46:18.610 --> 00:46:21.040 align:middle line:84%
to all manufacturing
firms every five years,

00:46:21.040 --> 00:46:24.430 align:middle line:84%
asking them lots and
lots of questions.

00:46:24.430 --> 00:46:25.850 align:middle line:90%
What do they produce?

00:46:25.850 --> 00:46:30.670 align:middle line:84%
How much do they sell of every
different, narrowly defined

00:46:30.670 --> 00:46:31.700 align:middle line:90%
product?

00:46:31.700 --> 00:46:33.710 align:middle line:90%
How many employees do you have?

00:46:33.710 --> 00:46:35.810 align:middle line:84%
What's the value of your
plant and equipment?

00:46:35.810 --> 00:46:38.710 align:middle line:90%
How much electricity do you use?

00:46:38.710 --> 00:46:42.610 align:middle line:84%
So you get lots of
data on these firms.

00:46:42.610 --> 00:46:45.557 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes the accounting data
of what are all their costs

00:46:45.557 --> 00:46:46.390 align:middle line:90%
are hard to believe.

00:46:46.390 --> 00:46:49.120 align:middle line:84%
But one thing that's very good
in that data is, do you exist?

00:46:49.120 --> 00:46:50.620 align:middle line:90%
What's your address?

00:46:50.620 --> 00:46:53.410 align:middle line:90%
How many employees do you have?

00:46:53.410 --> 00:46:57.430 align:middle line:84%
And so this lets you-- if you
want to study entry and exit,

00:46:57.430 --> 00:47:01.000 align:middle line:84%
you can just-- there's a
windowless room at the NBER

00:47:01.000 --> 00:47:02.840 align:middle line:84%
where one can
access census data.

00:47:02.840 --> 00:47:05.260 align:middle line:84%
Now, as a graduate student,
one of the difficulties

00:47:05.260 --> 00:47:08.890 align:middle line:84%
is that you try to access
it through the NBER,

00:47:08.890 --> 00:47:11.470 align:middle line:84%
and there's a two-year
application process

00:47:11.470 --> 00:47:14.840 align:middle line:84%
to be approved as a special
sworn employee of the census.

00:47:14.840 --> 00:47:18.232 align:middle line:84%
And two years is a long
time for a graduate student.

00:47:18.232 --> 00:47:20.440 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if I should
say this on camera, I think,

00:47:20.440 --> 00:47:22.120 align:middle line:84%
but what people
often do if they want

00:47:22.120 --> 00:47:24.700 align:middle line:84%
to access census data is
they get a RA job working

00:47:24.700 --> 00:47:27.100 align:middle line:84%
with someone who has
access to the census data,

00:47:27.100 --> 00:47:30.070 align:middle line:84%
learn the census data well
enough that they can describe

00:47:30.070 --> 00:47:33.130 align:middle line:84%
all of the regressions and code
that they will run when they get

00:47:33.130 --> 00:47:35.920 align:middle line:84%
access to the census data, and
then they have all the programs

00:47:35.920 --> 00:47:38.680 align:middle line:84%
entirely written to
just push the run

00:47:38.680 --> 00:47:40.540 align:middle line:84%
button when their
access is eventually

00:47:40.540 --> 00:47:41.740 align:middle line:90%
approved two years later.

00:47:41.740 --> 00:47:43.690 align:middle line:84%
And then they can
write their paper

00:47:43.690 --> 00:47:45.730 align:middle line:84%
using the Census of
Manufacturers data.

00:47:45.730 --> 00:47:49.990 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, one thing that's
very good to do with census data

00:47:49.990 --> 00:47:56.840 align:middle line:84%
is you can get very good
statistics on, how many firms

00:47:56.840 --> 00:47:58.440 align:middle line:90%
enter every five years?

00:47:58.440 --> 00:48:00.320 align:middle line:90%
How many firms grow?

00:48:00.320 --> 00:48:02.730 align:middle line:84%
Of firms that enter
in a five-year period,

00:48:02.730 --> 00:48:05.747 align:middle line:84%
how many of them are still
there five years later?

00:48:05.747 --> 00:48:07.830 align:middle line:84%
Of the ones that are still
there five years later,

00:48:07.830 --> 00:48:08.997 align:middle line:90%
how many of them have grown?

00:48:08.997 --> 00:48:10.520 align:middle line:90%
How many of them have shrunk?

00:48:10.520 --> 00:48:13.100 align:middle line:84%
What's the relative
profitability

00:48:13.100 --> 00:48:15.530 align:middle line:84%
of the new firms
versus the old firms?

00:48:15.530 --> 00:48:22.370 align:middle line:84%
So there's a set of old
papers I used to teach here.

00:48:22.370 --> 00:48:27.080 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, the classic one
that's still on my syllabus is

00:48:27.080 --> 00:48:34.670 align:middle line:84%
Dunne, Roberts, and Samuelson
from the 1970s that first just

00:48:34.670 --> 00:48:37.610 align:middle line:84%
wrote lots and lots of
statistics on what entry

00:48:37.610 --> 00:48:39.480 align:middle line:84%
and exit looked like
in the census data.

00:48:39.480 --> 00:48:41.000 align:middle line:84%
But you could search
for things that

00:48:41.000 --> 00:48:43.320 align:middle line:84%
have cited it to find
many updates since then.

00:48:43.320 --> 00:48:45.740 align:middle line:84%
But let me say, some
of the stylized facts

00:48:45.740 --> 00:48:49.550 align:middle line:84%
here is that according to
the census data, at least,

00:48:49.550 --> 00:48:52.550 align:middle line:90%
there's a lot of entry and exit.

00:48:52.550 --> 00:48:57.330 align:middle line:84%
And I think in some
industries, we know this.

00:48:57.330 --> 00:48:59.580 align:middle line:84%
If you watch restaurants
or something like that,

00:48:59.580 --> 00:49:01.860 align:middle line:84%
you're aware that
new restaurants are

00:49:01.860 --> 00:49:05.440 align:middle line:84%
born all the time,
restaurants die all the time.

00:49:05.440 --> 00:49:08.010 align:middle line:84%
There's a tremendous
amount of entry and exit

00:49:08.010 --> 00:49:09.780 align:middle line:90%
into the restaurant business.

00:49:09.780 --> 00:49:12.840 align:middle line:84%
Well, the surprising result from
Dunne, Robertson, and Samuelson

00:49:12.840 --> 00:49:17.580 align:middle line:84%
is that lots of other businesses
seem to look like that too.

00:49:17.580 --> 00:49:20.340 align:middle line:84%
Now, it's not the
case that there's

00:49:20.340 --> 00:49:21.930 align:middle line:84%
a tremendous number
of new Apples

00:49:21.930 --> 00:49:24.395 align:middle line:90%
and Samsungs every five years.

00:49:24.395 --> 00:49:25.770 align:middle line:84%
But if you look
at the firms that

00:49:25.770 --> 00:49:28.770 align:middle line:84%
are classified into many
narrowly defined businesses,

00:49:28.770 --> 00:49:32.220 align:middle line:84%
it seems like many
more are like the--

00:49:32.220 --> 00:49:36.030 align:middle line:84%
many more are like the
restaurants than the Apple

00:49:36.030 --> 00:49:39.870 align:middle line:90%
and Samsung phone producers.

00:49:39.870 --> 00:49:43.410 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so what
Jovanovic's paper is about,

00:49:43.410 --> 00:49:46.860 align:middle line:84%
trying to discuss
basic framework for how

00:49:46.860 --> 00:49:51.990 align:middle line:84%
do we think about entry and
exit in a way that would explain

00:49:51.990 --> 00:49:53.970 align:middle line:84%
what's going on in
the census data,

00:49:53.970 --> 00:49:55.620 align:middle line:84%
when all these new
firms are coming in,

00:49:55.620 --> 00:49:59.195 align:middle line:84%
all these firms are exiting,
and growth patterns, and so on.

00:49:59.195 --> 00:50:02.760 align:middle line:90%


00:50:02.760 --> 00:50:07.210 align:middle line:84%
So classic model Jovanovic
is a macro-ish paper.

00:50:07.210 --> 00:50:10.110 align:middle line:84%
So there's a lot of this
assuming continuum of firms

00:50:10.110 --> 00:50:14.140 align:middle line:84%
and whatever and assuming that
everything works out evenly.

00:50:14.140 --> 00:50:16.320 align:middle line:84%
So it looks a little
different from I/O,

00:50:16.320 --> 00:50:18.720 align:middle line:90%
but I wanted to cover it.

00:50:18.720 --> 00:50:20.910 align:middle line:90%
So we start in this model--

00:50:20.910 --> 00:50:24.240 align:middle line:84%
imagine we have a continuum
of small potential entrants

00:50:24.240 --> 00:50:26.180 align:middle line:90%
that are out there.

00:50:26.180 --> 00:50:28.990 align:middle line:84%
And these firms are going
to have some fixed cost

00:50:28.990 --> 00:50:31.310 align:middle line:90%
K of entering the industry.

00:50:31.310 --> 00:50:33.447 align:middle line:84%
And then if they
enter the industry

00:50:33.447 --> 00:50:35.030 align:middle line:84%
and then they decide
to leave, they're

00:50:35.030 --> 00:50:38.730 align:middle line:84%
going to have some liquidation
value w that's less than K.

00:50:38.730 --> 00:50:40.670 align:middle line:84%
So you're a
restaurant, you enter,

00:50:40.670 --> 00:50:42.780 align:middle line:84%
you pay K to set
up your restaurant.

00:50:42.780 --> 00:50:46.670 align:middle line:84%
When you're done,
you can sell all

00:50:46.670 --> 00:50:48.460 align:middle line:84%
your tables and your
cooking equipment

00:50:48.460 --> 00:50:50.210 align:middle line:84%
and the leases and
whatever, all the money

00:50:50.210 --> 00:50:53.100 align:middle line:84%
you spent on advertising and
other things, that's sunk.

00:50:53.100 --> 00:50:55.730 align:middle line:84%
But there's some fraction of--
there's some amount w that you

00:50:55.730 --> 00:50:57.280 align:middle line:90%
can get back when you close up.

00:50:57.280 --> 00:51:00.550 align:middle line:90%


00:51:00.550 --> 00:51:03.140 align:middle line:84%
Jovanovic assumed that these
firms have unknown types

00:51:03.140 --> 00:51:04.160 align:middle line:90%
theta i.

00:51:04.160 --> 00:51:06.370 align:middle line:90%
You can think of theta i as--

00:51:06.370 --> 00:51:10.330 align:middle line:84%
well, it's like the inverse
of their productivity.

00:51:10.330 --> 00:51:16.370 align:middle line:84%
So theta i is distributed
normal 0 sigma theta squared.

00:51:16.370 --> 00:51:18.890 align:middle line:84%
This is how good
of a firm you are.

00:51:18.890 --> 00:51:21.610 align:middle line:84%
You could think of it as,
like in the restaurant case,

00:51:21.610 --> 00:51:24.340 align:middle line:84%
everybody thinks they
have food that people

00:51:24.340 --> 00:51:25.910 align:middle line:90%
are going to like the taste of.

00:51:25.910 --> 00:51:28.560 align:middle line:84%
But you never know until
you open your restaurant

00:51:28.560 --> 00:51:30.220 align:middle line:90%
and you see how popular it is.

00:51:30.220 --> 00:51:34.240 align:middle line:84%
So all these firms, they all
have some d well, in fact,

00:51:34.240 --> 00:51:35.080 align:middle line:90%
they all have--

00:51:35.080 --> 00:51:37.520 align:middle line:84%
in this model, they all
have exactly the same prior.

00:51:37.520 --> 00:51:39.850 align:middle line:84%
They all have the prior
that my theta i is

00:51:39.850 --> 00:51:41.870 align:middle line:90%
drawn from this distribution.

00:51:41.870 --> 00:51:43.930 align:middle line:84%
But the only way to
learn whether my theta

00:51:43.930 --> 00:51:46.600 align:middle line:84%
i is low, in which I'm going
to be a very profitable firm,

00:51:46.600 --> 00:51:49.240 align:middle line:84%
or high, in which I'm going to
be a very unprofitable firm,

00:51:49.240 --> 00:51:53.290 align:middle line:84%
is to enter, start selling
some-- open my restaurant,

00:51:53.290 --> 00:51:55.030 align:middle line:84%
start selling to
customers, see if people

00:51:55.030 --> 00:51:58.335 align:middle line:84%
come and are willing to pay my
prices, and I sell out or not.

00:51:58.335 --> 00:52:01.150 align:middle line:90%


00:52:01.150 --> 00:52:03.772 align:middle line:90%
So he does this with the thetas.

00:52:03.772 --> 00:52:05.230 align:middle line:84%
Rather than quality
of the food, he

00:52:05.230 --> 00:52:08.380 align:middle line:84%
does this by your cost of
producing as if it's your--

00:52:08.380 --> 00:52:09.740 align:middle line:90%
everyone's trying to come in.

00:52:09.740 --> 00:52:14.620 align:middle line:84%
They're all going to try to
produce some kind of steel.

00:52:14.620 --> 00:52:17.350 align:middle line:84%
And then some of them end up
being high-cost producers,

00:52:17.350 --> 00:52:19.060 align:middle line:84%
some end up being
low-cost producers,

00:52:19.060 --> 00:52:21.950 align:middle line:84%
and that's what determines
their profitability.

00:52:21.950 --> 00:52:24.910 align:middle line:84%
So firm i's period
t cost, there's

00:52:24.910 --> 00:52:30.280 align:middle line:84%
this some common function c of
qit that applies to everyone.

00:52:30.280 --> 00:52:35.200 align:middle line:84%
But then it's multiplied by f
of theta i plus epsilon "it",

00:52:35.200 --> 00:52:38.950 align:middle line:84%
where theta i is your
permanent type and epsilon "it"

00:52:38.950 --> 00:52:41.860 align:middle line:90%
is a shock.

00:52:41.860 --> 00:52:46.720 align:middle line:84%
And what he's imagining is that
f might be some function that

00:52:46.720 --> 00:52:47.655 align:middle line:90%
looks like this.

00:52:47.655 --> 00:52:57.720 align:middle line:90%


00:52:57.720 --> 00:53:00.610 align:middle line:90%
And so we'll make this 1.

00:53:00.610 --> 00:53:06.250 align:middle line:84%
So if my type is
exactly 0, then--

00:53:06.250 --> 00:53:08.090 align:middle line:90%
or when I make this--

00:53:08.090 --> 00:53:16.325 align:middle line:90%


00:53:16.325 --> 00:53:18.200 align:middle line:84%
There are extra parameters
in this model that

00:53:18.200 --> 00:53:19.570 align:middle line:90%
probably shouldn't be there.

00:53:19.570 --> 00:53:23.250 align:middle line:90%
So let's call this theta 0.

00:53:23.250 --> 00:53:32.660 align:middle line:84%
So if my type is exactly
theta 0, my cost is c of q.

00:53:32.660 --> 00:53:36.320 align:middle line:84%
If my theta is
negative infinity,

00:53:36.320 --> 00:53:40.680 align:middle line:90%
then my cost is like 1/2 c of q.

00:53:40.680 --> 00:53:45.180 align:middle line:84%
And if my theta is like
positive infinity--

00:53:45.180 --> 00:53:49.010 align:middle line:90%
so I'm graphing f of x--

00:53:49.010 --> 00:53:52.550 align:middle line:90%
maybe my cost is twice c of q.

00:53:52.550 --> 00:53:55.160 align:middle line:84%
And so with these firms
that are entering,

00:53:55.160 --> 00:53:59.050 align:middle line:84%
if their costs are low, they're
going to earn positive profits,

00:53:59.050 --> 00:53:59.820 align:middle line:90%
high profits.

00:53:59.820 --> 00:54:03.680 align:middle line:84%
If their costs are high, they're
going to earn negative profits.

00:54:03.680 --> 00:54:06.740 align:middle line:84%
But he adds this
random shock in.

00:54:06.740 --> 00:54:09.000 align:middle line:84%
So there's this random
period-by-period shock.

00:54:09.000 --> 00:54:12.210 align:middle line:84%
So I enter, I try to
produce, this period

00:54:12.210 --> 00:54:14.430 align:middle line:84%
I turn out that
I had high costs.

00:54:14.430 --> 00:54:16.580 align:middle line:84%
I don't know, am I
a high theta type?

00:54:16.580 --> 00:54:18.260 align:middle line:90%
Or was I just unlucky?

00:54:18.260 --> 00:54:22.100 align:middle line:84%
So again, if you think about
it on the quality side,

00:54:22.100 --> 00:54:25.400 align:middle line:90%
I enter, I start selling--

00:54:25.400 --> 00:54:28.520 align:middle line:84%
either this week, there's
nobody at my restaurant; is it

00:54:28.520 --> 00:54:29.570 align:middle line:90%
that my quality is low?

00:54:29.570 --> 00:54:34.610 align:middle line:84%
Or did I just have a bad shock,
a bad random draw that week?

00:54:34.610 --> 00:54:39.050 align:middle line:90%
And so firms are--

00:54:39.050 --> 00:54:42.200 align:middle line:84%
symmetric information model
firms don't know their types.

00:54:42.200 --> 00:54:45.530 align:middle line:84%
But every time they
produce, they get a signal

00:54:45.530 --> 00:54:47.400 align:middle line:90%
about what their type is.

00:54:47.400 --> 00:54:49.010 align:middle line:90%
They update their beliefs.

00:54:49.010 --> 00:54:51.440 align:middle line:84%
And then by updating
their beliefs,

00:54:51.440 --> 00:54:56.720 align:middle line:84%
they're going to
change two things.

00:54:56.720 --> 00:54:59.520 align:middle line:90%
c is convex in this model.

00:54:59.520 --> 00:55:04.890 align:middle line:84%
So if I think my cost is low,
I'm going to produce a lot

00:55:04.890 --> 00:55:07.950 align:middle line:84%
and continue going because
I'm like, I can produce a lot

00:55:07.950 --> 00:55:11.010 align:middle line:84%
and earn positive profits at
the equilibrium level p star.

00:55:11.010 --> 00:55:12.953 align:middle line:84%
If my cost is high,
I'm only going

00:55:12.953 --> 00:55:15.120 align:middle line:84%
to produce a little because
I want to get the signal

00:55:15.120 --> 00:55:16.453 align:middle line:90%
and find out if I'm good or not.

00:55:16.453 --> 00:55:19.170 align:middle line:84%
But I'm only going to produce a
little bit because I'm probably

00:55:19.170 --> 00:55:21.003 align:middle line:84%
going to lose money on
everything I produce.

00:55:21.003 --> 00:55:23.362 align:middle line:90%


00:55:23.362 --> 00:55:25.460 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, he
treats the entrance

00:55:25.460 --> 00:55:27.540 align:middle line:90%
as a continuum per period.

00:55:27.540 --> 00:55:30.600 align:middle line:84%
And so with this
continuum of entrance,

00:55:30.600 --> 00:55:34.870 align:middle line:84%
you get a deterministic
price sequence, p1, p2, p3.

00:55:34.870 --> 00:55:37.320 align:middle line:84%
And he also assumes that the
firms are all price takers,

00:55:37.320 --> 00:55:42.150 align:middle line:84%
and they're just choosing q to
maximize the expectation over

00:55:42.150 --> 00:55:47.400 align:middle line:84%
theta i of the profits I'm going
to get this period, which is pt,

00:55:47.400 --> 00:55:53.010 align:middle line:84%
which I know, times q minus
my cost of production, c of q,

00:55:53.010 --> 00:55:54.570 align:middle line:90%
f of theta i plus epsilon "it".

00:55:54.570 --> 00:55:57.112 align:middle line:84%
And again, there's
an expectation here

00:55:57.112 --> 00:55:58.320 align:middle line:90%
because I don't know theta i.

00:55:58.320 --> 00:56:00.570 align:middle line:84%
And actually, also there's
going to be an epsilon "it"

00:56:00.570 --> 00:56:02.185 align:middle line:84%
is also going to
be random shock.

00:56:02.185 --> 00:56:04.560 align:middle line:84%
So I guess this really should
be expectation over theta i

00:56:04.560 --> 00:56:05.280 align:middle line:90%
and epsilon "it".

00:56:05.280 --> 00:56:13.650 align:middle line:90%


00:56:13.650 --> 00:56:18.395 align:middle line:84%
So then what's the optimal
behavior for a firm?

00:56:18.395 --> 00:56:21.020 align:middle line:90%


00:56:21.020 --> 00:56:24.150 align:middle line:84%
Suppose that you think
theta is very low.

00:56:24.150 --> 00:56:26.685 align:middle line:84%
If you think theta
is very low, then you

00:56:26.685 --> 00:56:28.435 align:middle line:84%
think your costs are
going to be very low.

00:56:28.435 --> 00:56:30.300 align:middle line:90%
You know that the prices are.

00:56:30.300 --> 00:56:32.177 align:middle line:84%
So you produce a
very high quantity--

00:56:32.177 --> 00:56:34.010 align:middle line:84%
because you can produce
a very high quantity

00:56:34.010 --> 00:56:37.160 align:middle line:84%
with the increasing costs
and still make a profit.

00:56:37.160 --> 00:56:40.440 align:middle line:84%
So this is not a constant
marginal cost model.

00:56:40.440 --> 00:56:42.650 align:middle line:90%
This is a marginal cost--

00:56:42.650 --> 00:56:45.160 align:middle line:84%
this is c of q that's
increasing in q.

00:56:45.160 --> 00:56:47.920 align:middle line:90%


00:56:47.920 --> 00:56:53.260 align:middle line:84%
If I have a medium
expectation of theta,

00:56:53.260 --> 00:56:55.920 align:middle line:84%
then I'm going to produce
some low quantity.

00:56:55.920 --> 00:56:58.392 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to hope that I'm
better than I think I am,

00:56:58.392 --> 00:57:00.100 align:middle line:84%
but I'm just going to
produce the units I

00:57:00.100 --> 00:57:03.940 align:middle line:84%
can produce cheaply, produce
my initial cheap units,

00:57:03.940 --> 00:57:06.417 align:middle line:84%
and then leave it at that
and then hope that I find out

00:57:06.417 --> 00:57:08.500 align:middle line:84%
I'm better, but at least
make a profit on the ones

00:57:08.500 --> 00:57:09.125 align:middle line:90%
that I produce.

00:57:09.125 --> 00:57:11.760 align:middle line:90%


00:57:11.760 --> 00:57:14.910 align:middle line:90%
If I have a high theta--

00:57:14.910 --> 00:57:20.070 align:middle line:84%
if I have a high expectation of
theta, I think I'm a bad firm,

00:57:20.070 --> 00:57:23.010 align:middle line:84%
but I'm uncertain
about how bad I am,

00:57:23.010 --> 00:57:25.800 align:middle line:84%
then I'm going to produce a
low qit and stay in the market.

00:57:25.800 --> 00:57:28.770 align:middle line:84%
And I'm just going to
produce these very few units

00:57:28.770 --> 00:57:30.900 align:middle line:84%
and try to sell those
and hope that I'm

00:57:30.900 --> 00:57:32.940 align:middle line:90%
better than I think I am.

00:57:32.940 --> 00:57:38.130 align:middle line:84%
But if I'm high theta and
I have low uncertainty,

00:57:38.130 --> 00:57:40.320 align:middle line:84%
then I'm just going to take
the liquidation value w

00:57:40.320 --> 00:57:42.430 align:middle line:90%
and get out of the market.

00:57:42.430 --> 00:57:45.440 align:middle line:90%
OK, so firms are making this--

00:57:45.440 --> 00:57:51.215 align:middle line:84%
endogenously choosing both,
What quantity do I produce?

00:57:51.215 --> 00:57:52.340 align:middle line:90%
What quantity do I produce?

00:57:52.340 --> 00:57:55.770 align:middle line:90%
And do I exit or not?

00:57:55.770 --> 00:57:59.370 align:middle line:84%
And he separates these things
where the amount I'll earn

00:57:59.370 --> 00:58:02.102 align:middle line:84%
doesn't depend on the
amount that I produced.

00:58:02.102 --> 00:58:03.810 align:middle line:84%
And so if I produce,
I'm going to produce

00:58:03.810 --> 00:58:05.405 align:middle line:90%
the static optimal quantity.

00:58:05.405 --> 00:58:09.260 align:middle line:90%


00:58:09.260 --> 00:58:09.920 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:58:09.920 --> 00:58:12.560 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So someone's belief
or their expectation of theta i

00:58:12.560 --> 00:58:15.245 align:middle line:84%
is just based off their previous
observations of theta i?

00:58:15.245 --> 00:58:17.120 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: It's
based on their previous--

00:58:17.120 --> 00:58:20.210 align:middle line:84%
like, every period you get an
observation of theta i plus

00:58:20.210 --> 00:58:21.170 align:middle line:90%
epsilon "it".

00:58:21.170 --> 00:58:24.330 align:middle line:84%
So you have a-- it's a
normal learning model.

00:58:24.330 --> 00:58:28.280 align:middle line:84%
I have a prior that
it's normal 0 theta.

00:58:28.280 --> 00:58:30.450 align:middle line:90%
These epsilons are also normal.

00:58:30.450 --> 00:58:32.480 align:middle line:84%
So every time I enter,
I get a normal signal,

00:58:32.480 --> 00:58:34.520 align:middle line:84%
and then I just Bayesian
update up and down.

00:58:34.520 --> 00:58:36.470 align:middle line:84%
And the number of
signals that I get

00:58:36.470 --> 00:58:41.650 align:middle line:84%
determines whether my posterior
is diffuse or narrow or tight.

00:58:41.650 --> 00:58:45.130 align:middle line:84%
So sometimes the older I am,
the less uncertainty I have.

00:58:45.130 --> 00:58:49.645 align:middle line:84%
Like, with normal distributions,
it's just purely 1 over N.

00:58:49.645 --> 00:58:51.730 align:middle line:84%
The uncertainty
diminishes like 1 over N

00:58:51.730 --> 00:58:53.330 align:middle line:90%
as I get N observations.

00:58:53.330 --> 00:58:58.280 align:middle line:90%


00:58:58.280 --> 00:59:03.350 align:middle line:84%
And so we notice that
a basic model like this

00:59:03.350 --> 00:59:07.460 align:middle line:84%
makes a number of predictions
about firm dynamics

00:59:07.460 --> 00:59:10.610 align:middle line:84%
that seem to fit with what
the basic census data on firm

00:59:10.610 --> 00:59:12.110 align:middle line:90%
dynamics look like.

00:59:12.110 --> 00:59:16.640 align:middle line:84%
So some observations,
small firms

00:59:16.640 --> 00:59:20.570 align:middle line:90%
grow faster and fail more often.

00:59:20.570 --> 00:59:21.600 align:middle line:90%
Why is that?

00:59:21.600 --> 00:59:24.770 align:middle line:84%
Well, a lot of the
small firms are--

00:59:24.770 --> 00:59:28.220 align:middle line:90%


00:59:28.220 --> 00:59:30.560 align:middle line:84%
a lot of the small
firms are these people

00:59:30.560 --> 00:59:32.750 align:middle line:90%
with high uncertainty.

00:59:32.750 --> 00:59:35.090 align:middle line:84%
The small firms are
new firms that come in

00:59:35.090 --> 00:59:38.550 align:middle line:84%
or firms that have come in
and gotten one bad draw.

00:59:38.550 --> 00:59:40.530 align:middle line:84%
They think their theta
is high, but they're

00:59:40.530 --> 00:59:42.480 align:middle line:84%
very uncertain
about their theta.

00:59:42.480 --> 00:59:46.140 align:middle line:84%
So they're producing
small quantities.

00:59:46.140 --> 00:59:48.210 align:middle line:84%
If they find out that
they're better firms,

00:59:48.210 --> 00:59:50.740 align:middle line:90%
they grow very quickly.

00:59:50.740 --> 00:59:54.880 align:middle line:84%
These guys, the only way you can
have a low expectation of theta

00:59:54.880 --> 00:59:56.540 align:middle line:84%
is if you've gotten
a lot of data

00:59:56.540 --> 00:59:59.620 align:middle line:84%
and all the data says that
your quantity is low--

00:59:59.620 --> 01:00:00.918 align:middle line:90%
your costs are low.

01:00:00.918 --> 01:00:03.460 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, you're not going to
grow very fast, because you've

01:00:03.460 --> 01:00:05.030 align:middle line:90%
already got a lot of data.

01:00:05.030 --> 01:00:07.447 align:middle line:84%
You're going to continue to
think that your costs are low,

01:00:07.447 --> 01:00:09.850 align:middle line:84%
and you're going to have
roughly the same output.

01:00:09.850 --> 01:00:15.070 align:middle line:84%
So the small firms are firms
who have a lot of uncertainty

01:00:15.070 --> 01:00:17.570 align:middle line:84%
but think their costs
are relatively high.

01:00:17.570 --> 01:00:21.290 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, they grow fast
if they stay in the market.

01:00:21.290 --> 01:00:24.105 align:middle line:84%
But they also just exit
and fail much more often.

01:00:24.105 --> 01:00:27.520 align:middle line:90%


01:00:27.520 --> 01:00:29.840 align:middle line:84%
Bigger firms have
higher profits.

01:00:29.840 --> 01:00:33.970 align:middle line:84%
So profits are quite
different across firms.

01:00:33.970 --> 01:00:35.920 align:middle line:84%
The high-profit
firms are the people

01:00:35.920 --> 01:00:37.370 align:middle line:90%
who've been around for a while.

01:00:37.370 --> 01:00:39.400 align:middle line:84%
They've learned that
their theta is very low.

01:00:39.400 --> 01:00:43.870 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, they can produce way
out here on their cost curve

01:00:43.870 --> 01:00:45.500 align:middle line:90%
and still make a profit.

01:00:45.500 --> 01:00:47.170 align:middle line:84%
And they're making
all these profits

01:00:47.170 --> 01:00:49.030 align:middle line:90%
on the inframarginal units.

01:00:49.030 --> 01:00:54.280 align:middle line:84%
So the big firms are the-- big
firms are the profitable firms,

01:00:54.280 --> 01:00:56.222 align:middle line:84%
and they're profitable
because they just

01:00:56.222 --> 01:00:58.305 align:middle line:84%
are more efficient cost-wise
than the other firms.

01:00:58.305 --> 01:01:01.460 align:middle line:90%


01:01:01.460 --> 01:01:08.750 align:middle line:84%
If you think about how
our industry concentration

01:01:08.750 --> 01:01:11.090 align:middle line:90%
and profits related--

01:01:11.090 --> 01:01:12.740 align:middle line:84%
and we have this
sort of standard view

01:01:12.740 --> 01:01:14.740 align:middle line:84%
that industry concentration and
profits are related because we

01:01:14.740 --> 01:01:17.120 align:middle line:84%
can put more firms into a
Cournot model or more firms

01:01:17.120 --> 01:01:22.800 align:middle line:84%
into a Hotelling circle
model, markups drop.

01:01:22.800 --> 01:01:31.290 align:middle line:84%
Here, this is like a
perfect competition model.

01:01:31.290 --> 01:01:34.740 align:middle line:84%
But in equilibrium,
industry concentration

01:01:34.740 --> 01:01:37.350 align:middle line:84%
is correlated in the
cross-section with high profits

01:01:37.350 --> 01:01:40.080 align:middle line:84%
because an industry
that happens to have

01:01:40.080 --> 01:01:43.350 align:middle line:84%
a few very low-cost
firms in it, those firms

01:01:43.350 --> 01:01:44.860 align:middle line:90%
will earn very high profits.

01:01:44.860 --> 01:01:47.010 align:middle line:84%
Whereas with another
industry, all the firms

01:01:47.010 --> 01:01:49.237 align:middle line:84%
have very similar
costs, then you're

01:01:49.237 --> 01:01:51.570 align:middle line:84%
just going to have a large
number of firms, each earning

01:01:51.570 --> 01:01:53.470 align:middle line:90%
individually low profits.

01:01:53.470 --> 01:01:55.230 align:middle line:84%
And so the firms that
are going to look

01:01:55.230 --> 01:01:57.000 align:middle line:84%
like high-profit
industries, they're

01:01:57.000 --> 01:01:59.760 align:middle line:84%
concentrated because they're
the firms that just have

01:01:59.760 --> 01:02:01.740 align:middle line:84%
a few firms that
have theta draws

01:02:01.740 --> 01:02:04.090 align:middle line:84%
much lower than everybody
else's theta draw.

01:02:04.090 --> 01:02:07.650 align:middle line:84%
And so you get this
correlation between profits

01:02:07.650 --> 01:02:11.820 align:middle line:84%
and concentration that
comes from the distribution

01:02:11.820 --> 01:02:14.740 align:middle line:90%
of realized costs.

01:02:14.740 --> 01:02:17.970 align:middle line:84%
So we get a profit
concentration cross-section,

01:02:17.970 --> 01:02:22.140 align:middle line:84%
but it's caused by a correlation
with the omitted variable

01:02:22.140 --> 01:02:24.690 align:middle line:84%
of distribution of
firm costs rather than

01:02:24.690 --> 01:02:26.495 align:middle line:84%
caused by competition
and causal effect.

01:02:26.495 --> 01:02:30.880 align:middle line:90%


01:02:30.880 --> 01:02:33.730 align:middle line:84%
Industry concentration
is also correlated

01:02:33.730 --> 01:02:36.290 align:middle line:84%
with cross-sectional
variance in profits.

01:02:36.290 --> 01:02:39.460 align:middle line:84%
So again, if you have
an industry that has--

01:02:39.460 --> 01:02:42.580 align:middle line:84%
if I do histograms of costs,
if I have an industry that

01:02:42.580 --> 01:02:47.020 align:middle line:84%
has a mass of firms that
have very low costs and then

01:02:47.020 --> 01:02:49.930 align:middle line:84%
some firms that
have higher costs,

01:02:49.930 --> 01:02:52.180 align:middle line:84%
this industry is going to
end up very concentrated

01:02:52.180 --> 01:02:54.530 align:middle line:84%
because the low-cost firms
are going to produce a ton.

01:02:54.530 --> 01:02:56.720 align:middle line:84%
All these firms are going
to produce small amounts.

01:02:56.720 --> 01:02:59.590 align:middle line:84%
And so we're going to see
highly profitable firms and very

01:02:59.590 --> 01:03:01.920 align:middle line:90%
nonprofitable firms.

01:03:01.920 --> 01:03:05.010 align:middle line:84%
If you're going to
have an industry that's

01:03:05.010 --> 01:03:06.810 align:middle line:90%
not concentrated--

01:03:06.810 --> 01:03:08.550 align:middle line:84%
an industry that's
not concentrated

01:03:08.550 --> 01:03:13.560 align:middle line:84%
is one that has much
less cost heterogeneity.

01:03:13.560 --> 01:03:16.050 align:middle line:84%
And so if it's got much
less cost heterogeneity,

01:03:16.050 --> 01:03:18.340 align:middle line:84%
no one produces
a large quantity.

01:03:18.340 --> 01:03:20.040 align:middle line:84%
And that also means
that everyone's

01:03:20.040 --> 01:03:22.020 align:middle line:84%
getting similar profits
to everybody else

01:03:22.020 --> 01:03:26.700 align:middle line:84%
because their costs are all
similar to everybody else.

01:03:26.700 --> 01:03:29.770 align:middle line:84%
Note that these guys are all
making essentially zero profits.

01:03:29.770 --> 01:03:31.770 align:middle line:84%
And so it's like
small numbers and 0,

01:03:31.770 --> 01:03:35.490 align:middle line:84%
but small numbers and 0 are
not so different in this model.

01:03:35.490 --> 01:03:37.910 align:middle line:90%
So anyway, you get this.

01:03:37.910 --> 01:03:41.390 align:middle line:84%
Concentration is both correlated
with levels of profits

01:03:41.390 --> 01:03:44.810 align:middle line:84%
and with cross-sectional
variance in profits.

01:03:44.810 --> 01:03:52.050 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so there's a
large, large literature

01:03:52.050 --> 01:03:54.660 align:middle line:84%
on the empirics of
entry that bears out

01:03:54.660 --> 01:03:56.770 align:middle line:84%
that many of these
things seem to be true.

01:03:56.770 --> 01:03:59.640 align:middle line:84%
And I guess this
is the way we often

01:03:59.640 --> 01:04:01.440 align:middle line:84%
think about-- we make
sense of that data,

01:04:01.440 --> 01:04:04.370 align:middle line:84%
is that it's not that all
firms have the same technology.

01:04:04.370 --> 01:04:08.100 align:middle line:84%
Some firms are just more
efficient than others

01:04:08.100 --> 01:04:10.520 align:middle line:84%
for no observable reason--
for no observable reason.

01:04:10.520 --> 01:04:17.970 align:middle line:90%


01:04:17.970 --> 01:04:20.260 align:middle line:90%
Any questions on this framework?

01:04:20.260 --> 01:04:22.510 align:middle line:84%
I think that's all
I had to say on it.

01:04:22.510 --> 01:04:23.050 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

01:04:23.050 --> 01:04:25.930 align:middle line:84%
So what I want to do in this
lecture-- and I covered this--

01:04:25.930 --> 01:04:28.540 align:middle line:90%
I don't always teach Jovanovic.

01:04:28.540 --> 01:04:35.890 align:middle line:84%
But I like to have some recent
theoretical papers in the class.

01:04:35.890 --> 01:04:38.860 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, this is a
paper by Nikhil Vellodi,

01:04:38.860 --> 01:04:41.770 align:middle line:84%
who's an assistant
professor in Paris.

01:04:41.770 --> 01:04:46.000 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so basically what
Vellodi has done in this paper

01:04:46.000 --> 01:04:52.780 align:middle line:84%
is gone back to Jovanovic
1982 and used Jovanovic 1982

01:04:52.780 --> 01:04:56.680 align:middle line:84%
to think about the effects
of rating intermediaries

01:04:56.680 --> 01:05:00.580 align:middle line:84%
and what types of policies
we'd want rating intermediaries

01:05:00.580 --> 01:05:02.810 align:middle line:84%
to have or what effects
rating intermediaries have.

01:05:02.810 --> 01:05:06.772 align:middle line:84%
So by rating
intermediaries, he has--

01:05:06.772 --> 01:05:09.790 align:middle line:84%
thinking about firms
like Amazon and Yelp,

01:05:09.790 --> 01:05:13.510 align:middle line:84%
where on Amazon, you can
leave feedback-- like Amazon,

01:05:13.510 --> 01:05:15.160 align:middle line:84%
majority of the
merchandise on Amazon

01:05:15.160 --> 01:05:17.840 align:middle line:84%
is not actually sold by--
it's not by Amazon itself.

01:05:17.840 --> 01:05:21.370 align:middle line:84%
It's sold by third
parties selling on Amazon.

01:05:21.370 --> 01:05:24.500 align:middle line:84%
There are lots of-- you want
to buy an umbrella on Amazon,

01:05:24.500 --> 01:05:27.093 align:middle line:84%
you go there and you look at
the reviews of that umbrella

01:05:27.093 --> 01:05:29.260 align:middle line:84%
and whether people say that
umbrella is good or bad,

01:05:29.260 --> 01:05:31.360 align:middle line:90%
and then you buy it or not.

01:05:31.360 --> 01:05:34.180 align:middle line:84%
You get Yelp or
Tripadvisor or whomever

01:05:34.180 --> 01:05:37.097 align:middle line:84%
that has lots of reviews of
restaurants and tells you,

01:05:37.097 --> 01:05:38.180 align:middle line:90%
Is this a good restaurant?

01:05:38.180 --> 01:05:39.690 align:middle line:90%
Is this a bad restaurant?

01:05:39.690 --> 01:05:41.865 align:middle line:84%
Consumers use that to learn
about product quality.

01:05:41.865 --> 01:05:44.900 align:middle line:90%


01:05:44.900 --> 01:05:49.160 align:middle line:84%
And so there's this sort
of challenge in coming up

01:05:49.160 --> 01:05:51.110 align:middle line:84%
with these in this
business model

01:05:51.110 --> 01:05:59.080 align:middle line:84%
is that there are big firms
that everybody buys from.

01:05:59.080 --> 01:06:00.830 align:middle line:84%
On the big firms
everybody buys from,

01:06:00.830 --> 01:06:02.440 align:middle line:84%
there are going to be a
zillion reviews and people are

01:06:02.440 --> 01:06:04.910 align:middle line:84%
going to be able to find out
the quality of those firms.

01:06:04.910 --> 01:06:07.630 align:middle line:84%
But you have many,
many small entrants

01:06:07.630 --> 01:06:10.687 align:middle line:84%
and many, many new entrants
all the time into these models.

01:06:10.687 --> 01:06:12.520 align:middle line:84%
And it's the new entrants
that people really

01:06:12.520 --> 01:06:14.978 align:middle line:84%
want to know about and learn
about whether their quality is

01:06:14.978 --> 01:06:15.940 align:middle line:90%
good or bad.

01:06:15.940 --> 01:06:18.310 align:middle line:84%
But there's this effect
of if you have new firms,

01:06:18.310 --> 01:06:21.010 align:middle line:84%
people are hesitant to buy
from them because they're new.

01:06:21.010 --> 01:06:22.930 align:middle line:84%
And because people are hesitant
to buy from them because they're

01:06:22.930 --> 01:06:24.370 align:middle line:90%
new, there are very few reviews.

01:06:24.370 --> 01:06:26.078 align:middle line:84%
And therefore there
are very few reviews,

01:06:26.078 --> 01:06:27.800 align:middle line:84%
therefore people can't
learn about them.

01:06:27.800 --> 01:06:29.320 align:middle line:84%
And so he wants
to think about is,

01:06:29.320 --> 01:06:34.300 align:middle line:84%
what would be the optimal
design for a rating intermediary

01:06:34.300 --> 01:06:39.290 align:middle line:84%
that wanted to promote an
efficient level of competition?

01:06:39.290 --> 01:06:42.190 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, we
think there could be--

01:06:42.190 --> 01:06:44.182 align:middle line:84%
why could there be too
little entry in models?

01:06:44.182 --> 01:06:46.390 align:middle line:84%
There could be too little
entry here-- another reason

01:06:46.390 --> 01:06:48.280 align:middle line:84%
I didn't put my theory
in my first part was

01:06:48.280 --> 01:06:50.350 align:middle line:84%
there'd be too little entry
because people don't know

01:06:50.350 --> 01:06:52.183 align:middle line:84%
the quality of new
firms, and therefore they

01:06:52.183 --> 01:06:54.220 align:middle line:84%
won't buy from new firms,
and therefore no one

01:06:54.220 --> 01:06:58.510 align:middle line:84%
will enter and have people
learn about their quality.

01:06:58.510 --> 01:07:00.343 align:middle line:84%
Jovanovic was very
convenient that the firms

01:07:00.343 --> 01:07:02.427 align:middle line:84%
could enter and then produce
these tiny quantities

01:07:02.427 --> 01:07:04.720 align:middle line:84%
and get highly informative
signals about their quality

01:07:04.720 --> 01:07:07.450 align:middle line:84%
and know whether to
start producing more.

01:07:07.450 --> 01:07:11.230 align:middle line:84%
In practice, that's not
something restaurants

01:07:11.230 --> 01:07:13.450 align:middle line:84%
can do, is come in and
produce a tiny quantity

01:07:13.450 --> 01:07:17.610 align:middle line:84%
and get a lot of
reviews on Yelp.

01:07:17.610 --> 01:07:20.070 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, models
got a continuum

01:07:20.070 --> 01:07:21.460 align:middle line:90%
of small potential entrants.

01:07:21.460 --> 01:07:23.280 align:middle line:84%
So think of these
as new restaurants

01:07:23.280 --> 01:07:26.130 align:middle line:84%
or new guys just trying to
sell black folding umbrellas

01:07:26.130 --> 01:07:27.840 align:middle line:90%
on Amazon.

01:07:27.840 --> 01:07:31.920 align:middle line:84%
They have a fixed
cost K of entering.

01:07:31.920 --> 01:07:34.587 align:middle line:84%
They're going to have some
flow cost of operation.

01:07:34.587 --> 01:07:35.920 align:middle line:90%
I didn't put a letter down here.

01:07:35.920 --> 01:07:36.630 align:middle line:90%
Maybe this is c.

01:07:36.630 --> 01:07:38.850 align:middle line:84%
But every period that I'm
continuing to operate,

01:07:38.850 --> 01:07:42.420 align:middle line:90%
I have to pay some flow cost.

01:07:42.420 --> 01:07:45.070 align:middle line:84%
Just to make the model have
a steady state equilibrium,

01:07:45.070 --> 01:07:47.040 align:middle line:84%
he has some exogenous
death rate delta.

01:07:47.040 --> 01:07:48.870 align:middle line:84%
So every firm with
some probability

01:07:48.870 --> 01:07:52.140 align:middle line:90%
just disappears in each period.

01:07:52.140 --> 01:07:58.110 align:middle line:84%
And then firms have
some discount rate rho.

01:07:58.110 --> 01:08:02.920 align:middle line:84%
In his model, he's
simplifying the theory

01:08:02.920 --> 01:08:05.320 align:middle line:84%
by assuming that it's
a discrete type model.

01:08:05.320 --> 01:08:09.860 align:middle line:84%
All restaurants have either
quality 0 or quality 1.

01:08:09.860 --> 01:08:13.190 align:middle line:84%
So it's just a pure
bimodal distribution.

01:08:13.190 --> 01:08:17.080 align:middle line:84%
Again, like in Jovanovic, it's
a symmetric information model.

01:08:17.080 --> 01:08:22.250 align:middle line:84%
There's prior mean p0 that
the firm has quality 1.

01:08:22.250 --> 01:08:24.680 align:middle line:84%
And that information is
symmetric among everyone

01:08:24.680 --> 01:08:25.290 align:middle line:90%
in the market.

01:08:25.290 --> 01:08:27.248 align:middle line:84%
No one knows or everyone
knows new restaurants,

01:08:27.248 --> 01:08:29.520 align:middle line:84%
there's probability p0
that their food is good,

01:08:29.520 --> 01:08:33.500 align:middle line:84%
probability 1 minus p0
that their food is bad.

01:08:33.500 --> 01:08:38.260 align:middle line:84%
Every firm has
exactly one table.

01:08:38.260 --> 01:08:44.590 align:middle line:84%
And the firms can do is set
prices qit for their table

01:08:44.590 --> 01:08:45.314 align:middle line:90%
at time t.

01:08:45.314 --> 01:08:49.700 align:middle line:90%


01:08:49.700 --> 01:08:53.990 align:middle line:84%
What he assumes is that
you have this prior p0,

01:08:53.990 --> 01:09:00.140 align:middle line:84%
and then you have a posterior--
every period, if somebody--

01:09:00.140 --> 01:09:04.130 align:middle line:84%
if you're operating,
then reviews

01:09:04.130 --> 01:09:06.740 align:middle line:84%
arrive according to
a Poisson process.

01:09:06.740 --> 01:09:10.250 align:middle line:84%
And those reviews are
going to make the quality,

01:09:10.250 --> 01:09:12.109 align:middle line:84%
the posterior quality
go up or down,

01:09:12.109 --> 01:09:14.540 align:middle line:84%
whether it's a good
review or a bad review.

01:09:14.540 --> 01:09:19.760 align:middle line:84%
He goes with this
continuous time,

01:09:19.760 --> 01:09:25.160 align:middle line:84%
Brownian motion learning
process where my posterior "pit"

01:09:25.160 --> 01:09:30.810 align:middle line:84%
evolves according to dp "it"
equals square root of lambda

01:09:30.810 --> 01:09:35.870 align:middle line:84%
"it" "pit" 1 minus
"pit" times dZit,

01:09:35.870 --> 01:09:38.390 align:middle line:90%
where Zit is a Brownian motion.

01:09:38.390 --> 01:09:42.200 align:middle line:84%
But you can roughly think
of this as lambda "it"

01:09:42.200 --> 01:09:44.510 align:middle line:90%
is the review arrival rate.

01:09:44.510 --> 01:09:47.779 align:middle line:84%
And each of these is
like a normal signal.

01:09:47.779 --> 01:09:49.680 align:middle line:84%
And so if it's a
normal signal, I'm

01:09:49.680 --> 01:09:52.229 align:middle line:84%
going to get information that
either pushes me to the right

01:09:52.229 --> 01:09:52.979 align:middle line:90%
or left.

01:09:52.979 --> 01:09:57.420 align:middle line:90%
And the variance of--

01:09:57.420 --> 01:09:58.980 align:middle line:84%
since I have a
Bernoulli quality,

01:09:58.980 --> 01:10:03.240 align:middle line:84%
the variance of my distribution
is just "pit" 1 minus "pit"

01:10:03.240 --> 01:10:05.490 align:middle line:90%
if it's Bernoulli probability p.

01:10:05.490 --> 01:10:09.330 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, the new
information is arriving,

01:10:09.330 --> 01:10:11.947 align:middle line:84%
and I'm getting square
root of lambda "it"

01:10:11.947 --> 01:10:14.280 align:middle line:84%
is how much the standard
deviation changes when you have

01:10:14.280 --> 01:10:15.630 align:middle line:90%
lambda "it" reviews.

01:10:15.630 --> 01:10:17.940 align:middle line:84%
And then dZit is a
Brownian motion that's

01:10:17.940 --> 01:10:19.272 align:middle line:90%
just positive or negative.

01:10:19.272 --> 01:10:20.730 align:middle line:84%
So you can think
of it as the limit

01:10:20.730 --> 01:10:24.270 align:middle line:84%
as you get a number of
normally distributed

01:10:24.270 --> 01:10:25.890 align:middle line:84%
signals saying
this one was good,

01:10:25.890 --> 01:10:29.460 align:middle line:84%
this one was bad, this good,
this bad, this good, this bad.

01:10:29.460 --> 01:10:32.935 align:middle line:84%
That pushes you back and forth
in like a Brownian motion

01:10:32.935 --> 01:10:33.435 align:middle line:90%
process.

01:10:33.435 --> 01:10:37.520 align:middle line:90%


01:10:37.520 --> 01:10:40.277 align:middle line:84%
And why is he doing
this in continuous time?

01:10:40.277 --> 01:10:42.860 align:middle line:84%
He's doing this in continuous
time instead of in discrete time

01:10:42.860 --> 01:10:45.170 align:middle line:84%
because it's a state
of the art thing,

01:10:45.170 --> 01:10:48.450 align:middle line:84%
but it's also that sometimes
continuous models are easier

01:10:48.450 --> 01:10:49.700 align:middle line:90%
to solve than discrete models.

01:10:49.700 --> 01:10:52.148 align:middle line:84%
You have to keep track of
what your posterior could be.

01:10:52.148 --> 01:10:54.440 align:middle line:84%
If you've had three and for
and five and seven signals,

01:10:54.440 --> 01:10:57.320 align:middle line:84%
the continuous model
is easier to solve.

01:10:57.320 --> 01:11:01.530 align:middle line:84%
So every consumer in each
period decides which--

01:11:01.530 --> 01:11:04.050 align:middle line:84%
so every consumer goes to
exactly one restaurant.

01:11:04.050 --> 01:11:07.250 align:middle line:90%
They decide which firm to visit.

01:11:07.250 --> 01:11:11.420 align:middle line:84%
If excess consumers visited
any type of firm, any firm,

01:11:11.420 --> 01:11:12.750 align:middle line:90%
then service is rationed.

01:11:12.750 --> 01:11:17.600 align:middle line:84%
There's a line outside, and
only some of the people go in.

01:11:17.600 --> 01:11:23.870 align:middle line:84%
Consumers get utility theta
i plus epsilon ij if they're

01:11:23.870 --> 01:11:25.295 align:middle line:90%
served by restaurant i.

01:11:25.295 --> 01:11:29.000 align:middle line:90%


01:11:29.000 --> 01:11:34.490 align:middle line:84%
So given that all the firm's
priors are common knowledge,

01:11:34.490 --> 01:11:39.290 align:middle line:84%
in equilibrium, everyone's going
to get served with probability 1

01:11:39.290 --> 01:11:42.570 align:middle line:84%
at firms that have a
rating of at least p tilde.

01:11:42.570 --> 01:11:47.280 align:middle line:84%
And if your prior is--
like, you have one table,

01:11:47.280 --> 01:11:48.410 align:middle line:90%
so it's going to be like--

01:11:48.410 --> 01:11:49.785 align:middle line:84%
you're going to
clear the market.

01:11:49.785 --> 01:11:52.610 align:middle line:84%
If your quality is high enough,
you're going to price at some--

01:11:52.610 --> 01:11:54.110 align:middle line:84%
if your quality is
at least p tilde,

01:11:54.110 --> 01:11:55.818 align:middle line:84%
you're going to price
at some equilibrium

01:11:55.818 --> 01:11:58.190 align:middle line:84%
price that gets people to
come to your restaurant.

01:11:58.190 --> 01:12:01.230 align:middle line:84%
And you're going to price it
exactly one customer comes.

01:12:01.230 --> 01:12:03.380 align:middle line:84%
If your price is attracting
multiple customers,

01:12:03.380 --> 01:12:05.880 align:middle line:84%
you should be raising your price
and getting fewer customers

01:12:05.880 --> 01:12:08.360 align:middle line:90%
until you fill your capacity.

01:12:08.360 --> 01:12:13.640 align:middle line:84%
At all higher prices,
everyone is going to be--

01:12:13.640 --> 01:12:17.550 align:middle line:84%
yeah, so you can tell Nikhil is
a theorist not an IO economist.

01:12:17.550 --> 01:12:20.270 align:middle line:84%
He's using q for prices
and p for qualities,

01:12:20.270 --> 01:12:23.280 align:middle line:84%
rather than p for prices
and s for qualities.

01:12:23.280 --> 01:12:26.900 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, the firms are
setting these prices q.

01:12:26.900 --> 01:12:30.980 align:middle line:84%
In equilibrium, the prices
are just p minus a constant.

01:12:30.980 --> 01:12:33.440 align:middle line:84%
And that makes consumers
indifferent to going

01:12:33.440 --> 01:12:36.470 align:middle line:84%
to all high-quality
restaurants and has

01:12:36.470 --> 01:12:40.760 align:middle line:84%
all high-quality restaurants
attracting exactly one customer.

01:12:40.760 --> 01:12:44.180 align:middle line:84%
Why you price it p minus
w, it's because there

01:12:44.180 --> 01:12:46.933 align:middle line:84%
are low-quality
restaurants that people

01:12:46.933 --> 01:12:48.350 align:middle line:84%
aren't going to
want to go to that

01:12:48.350 --> 01:12:49.645 align:middle line:90%
are willing to sell at a loss.

01:12:49.645 --> 01:12:51.020 align:middle line:84%
Because they sell
at a loss, they

01:12:51.020 --> 01:12:54.180 align:middle line:84%
get people to come
and review them.

01:12:54.180 --> 01:12:58.470 align:middle line:84%
So in his model,
basically the lambda "it",

01:12:58.470 --> 01:13:02.570 align:middle line:84%
the review arrival rate, is
going to be a sum of two terms.

01:13:02.570 --> 01:13:03.705 align:middle line:90%
It's going to be a sum.

01:13:03.705 --> 01:13:05.580 align:middle line:84%
Lambda is going to be
the number of customers

01:13:05.580 --> 01:13:09.630 align:middle line:84%
who visit your restaurant
because it's optimal to do so,

01:13:09.630 --> 01:13:10.660 align:middle line:90%
plus epsilon.

01:13:10.660 --> 01:13:13.410 align:middle line:84%
So there's this epsilon
background rate of arrivals.

01:13:13.410 --> 01:13:15.510 align:middle line:84%
We'll call them tourists
from out of town

01:13:15.510 --> 01:13:17.880 align:middle line:84%
who have no idea what the
qualities are and just show up

01:13:17.880 --> 01:13:19.390 align:middle line:90%
randomly at your restaurant.

01:13:19.390 --> 01:13:22.890 align:middle line:84%
So most reviews come
from the one person

01:13:22.890 --> 01:13:25.270 align:middle line:84%
who knows your quality
and buys there.

01:13:25.270 --> 01:13:29.410 align:middle line:84%
But there is this
epsilon arrival rate.

01:13:29.410 --> 01:13:33.250 align:middle line:84%
The firms that are hanging
around that have low quality,

01:13:33.250 --> 01:13:35.620 align:middle line:84%
they would like people to
come to their restaurant

01:13:35.620 --> 01:13:37.960 align:middle line:84%
and review it so they can
learn if their quality is high

01:13:37.960 --> 01:13:40.418 align:middle line:84%
and they can start charging
more money for their restaurant

01:13:40.418 --> 01:13:43.690 align:middle line:84%
if it's high or exit if
their quality is low.

01:13:43.690 --> 01:13:46.810 align:middle line:84%
But there's only so much
they're willing to pay.

01:13:46.810 --> 01:13:49.313 align:middle line:84%
So they're willing to price
below cost to get people

01:13:49.313 --> 01:13:50.480 align:middle line:90%
to come to their restaurant.

01:13:50.480 --> 01:13:52.355 align:middle line:84%
But there's only so much
you're willing to do

01:13:52.355 --> 01:13:55.000 align:middle line:84%
that because, at some point,
you're just better off exiting.

01:13:55.000 --> 01:13:59.350 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, so that's why
you have this positive w.

01:13:59.350 --> 01:14:02.890 align:middle line:84%
That is, everybody
is giving consumers

01:14:02.890 --> 01:14:05.932 align:middle line:84%
some surplus because the guys
who are on the outside looking

01:14:05.932 --> 01:14:08.140 align:middle line:84%
in are going to give the
consumer some surplus to try

01:14:08.140 --> 01:14:10.690 align:middle line:90%
to get the customers to come.

01:14:10.690 --> 01:14:13.630 align:middle line:84%
OK, so I'm going to write V
of p for the value function

01:14:13.630 --> 01:14:16.660 align:middle line:90%
of a firm that has posterior p.

01:14:16.660 --> 01:14:19.480 align:middle line:84%
Free entry implies
that V of p0 equals

01:14:19.480 --> 01:14:21.760 align:middle line:84%
K. When the firms
are entering, it

01:14:21.760 --> 01:14:23.590 align:middle line:84%
must be that their
lifetime expected

01:14:23.590 --> 01:14:26.200 align:middle line:84%
discounted value of profits
is exactly equal to the fixed

01:14:26.200 --> 01:14:28.240 align:middle line:90%
costs.

01:14:28.240 --> 01:14:31.810 align:middle line:84%
And then exit occurs when
your posterior gets down

01:14:31.810 --> 01:14:35.410 align:middle line:84%
to p lower bar, where
V of p lower bar is 0.

01:14:35.410 --> 01:14:38.620 align:middle line:84%
And V of p lower bar is 0 in
this model without the scrappage

01:14:38.620 --> 01:14:41.256 align:middle line:84%
because there's this
flow cost of operation.

01:14:41.256 --> 01:14:45.633 align:middle line:90%


01:14:45.633 --> 01:14:48.050 align:middle line:84%
A complicated model, but I
thought what I would do is here

01:14:48.050 --> 01:14:49.675 align:middle line:84%
is just show you a
couple of the graphs

01:14:49.675 --> 01:14:52.580 align:middle line:84%
and talk through what
ends up happening.

01:14:52.580 --> 01:14:56.470 align:middle line:84%
So first observation--
so it's not-- yeah,

01:14:56.470 --> 01:15:01.780 align:middle line:84%
so if you're a new
firm and people--

01:15:01.780 --> 01:15:10.290 align:middle line:84%
in some sense, in equilibrium,
if the number of customers is x,

01:15:10.290 --> 01:15:14.010 align:middle line:84%
the x best restaurants are going
to all get one customer each.

01:15:14.010 --> 01:15:15.690 align:middle line:84%
And every restaurant
below x is going

01:15:15.690 --> 01:15:20.760 align:middle line:84%
to get no customers, other than
the randomly arriving tourists.

01:15:20.760 --> 01:15:24.330 align:middle line:84%
But firms-- if you're just
on the outside looking

01:15:24.330 --> 01:15:29.700 align:middle line:84%
in-- so if we have this sort
of posterior on my quality,

01:15:29.700 --> 01:15:31.690 align:middle line:84%
I think it was p
tilde I called it--

01:15:31.690 --> 01:15:33.730 align:middle line:84%
so everybody up here
fills their restaurant.

01:15:33.730 --> 01:15:37.530 align:middle line:84%
Everybody else here only gets
the epsilon flow of tourists.

01:15:37.530 --> 01:15:40.830 align:middle line:84%
If your quality is
right here, you're

01:15:40.830 --> 01:15:43.195 align:middle line:84%
willing to lose
some money in order

01:15:43.195 --> 01:15:45.570 align:middle line:84%
to get the customers to visit
your restaurant to find out

01:15:45.570 --> 01:15:47.820 align:middle line:84%
whether your restaurant
is good so you can

01:15:47.820 --> 01:15:49.830 align:middle line:90%
get in or out of the market.

01:15:49.830 --> 01:15:53.070 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, firms price somewhat
below cost to attract customers.

01:15:53.070 --> 01:15:57.030 align:middle line:84%
Because this guy is selling
at-- this guy is willing to sell

01:15:57.030 --> 01:15:58.950 align:middle line:90%
at a loss, everybody else has--

01:15:58.950 --> 01:16:02.700 align:middle line:84%
everybody else can only
charge their marginal value

01:16:02.700 --> 01:16:04.230 align:middle line:90%
above this guy--

01:16:04.230 --> 01:16:07.390 align:middle line:84%
their marginal quality
above that guy.

01:16:07.390 --> 01:16:11.030 align:middle line:84%
So consumers get--
consumers get some profit.

01:16:11.030 --> 01:16:12.920 align:middle line:90%
What is the consumer surplus?

01:16:12.920 --> 01:16:15.820 align:middle line:84%
The consumer surplus here
is the amount of money

01:16:15.820 --> 01:16:17.560 align:middle line:90%
this guy is willing to lose.

01:16:17.560 --> 01:16:21.490 align:middle line:84%
Because the amount of money that
this guy is willing to lose is--

01:16:21.490 --> 01:16:23.530 align:middle line:84%
the w is then
whatever what you get

01:16:23.530 --> 01:16:27.040 align:middle line:84%
at all higher-quality
restaurants.

01:16:27.040 --> 01:16:32.260 align:middle line:84%
With full information, learning
about new entrants is very slow.

01:16:32.260 --> 01:16:35.120 align:middle line:84%
And firms exit after they
just get some bad news.

01:16:35.120 --> 01:16:35.940 align:middle line:90%
So here is his--

01:16:35.940 --> 01:16:38.620 align:middle line:90%


01:16:38.620 --> 01:16:43.060 align:middle line:84%
this is an example
where p0, the prior,

01:16:43.060 --> 01:16:48.030 align:middle line:84%
is in the range where you're
not in the money on customers.

01:16:48.030 --> 01:16:54.260 align:middle line:84%
So at a p0 restaurant, you're
not getting very many--

01:16:54.260 --> 01:16:57.480 align:middle line:84%
you're out of the money, and
customers are not coming to you.

01:16:57.480 --> 01:16:59.900 align:middle line:84%
And so what we get
is, there's a lot

01:16:59.900 --> 01:17:04.930 align:middle line:84%
of firms who just entered about
who the prior quality is p0,

01:17:04.930 --> 01:17:07.450 align:middle line:84%
and only epsilon
people go to them.

01:17:07.450 --> 01:17:09.670 align:middle line:84%
And so we get a
lot of mass there

01:17:09.670 --> 01:17:11.650 align:middle line:84%
because when there are
only epsilon customers,

01:17:11.650 --> 01:17:14.200 align:middle line:84%
people don't learn
much, and your posterior

01:17:14.200 --> 01:17:16.910 align:middle line:84%
about their quality
doesn't move very fast.

01:17:16.910 --> 01:17:23.480 align:middle line:84%
If you get one or two bad
reviews, then you just exit.

01:17:23.480 --> 01:17:27.020 align:middle line:84%
So you start here, you
get a bad review or two,

01:17:27.020 --> 01:17:28.350 align:middle line:90%
you just exit the market.

01:17:28.350 --> 01:17:30.230 align:middle line:84%
And then also, there
aren't many firms

01:17:30.230 --> 01:17:33.560 align:middle line:84%
down here with these qualities
because two bad reviews,

01:17:33.560 --> 01:17:34.080 align:middle line:90%
you leave.

01:17:34.080 --> 01:17:35.663 align:middle line:84%
And then even if you
would have gotten

01:17:35.663 --> 01:17:38.160 align:middle line:84%
a third review coming back
up, you're not there anymore.

01:17:38.160 --> 01:17:41.130 align:middle line:90%


01:17:41.130 --> 01:17:44.400 align:middle line:84%
Then there are fewer
firms with qualities

01:17:44.400 --> 01:17:47.130 align:middle line:84%
here because once your
quality, your posterior

01:17:47.130 --> 01:17:50.680 align:middle line:84%
gets above p upper bar, people
learn about you very quickly.

01:17:50.680 --> 01:17:52.200 align:middle line:84%
People learn about
you very quickly,

01:17:52.200 --> 01:17:53.520 align:middle line:84%
your distribution
moves, and it either

01:17:53.520 --> 01:17:55.437 align:middle line:84%
moves back to this range
where no one's coming

01:17:55.437 --> 01:17:59.080 align:middle line:84%
to your restaurant, or it moves
up, and people learn it's good.

01:17:59.080 --> 01:18:01.090 align:middle line:84%
And in this model,
everyone is either 0 or 1.

01:18:01.090 --> 01:18:03.510 align:middle line:84%
So very quickly, people
learn that you're a quality 1

01:18:03.510 --> 01:18:06.010 align:middle line:90%
restaurant.

01:18:06.010 --> 01:18:08.200 align:middle line:84%
So we get this distribution
of firm qualities

01:18:08.200 --> 01:18:10.450 align:middle line:84%
where there are a lot of
firms that are having trouble

01:18:10.450 --> 01:18:12.190 align:middle line:90%
attracting anyone's reviews.

01:18:12.190 --> 01:18:15.130 align:middle line:84%
They stay, hang out here,
and their posteriors

01:18:15.130 --> 01:18:16.280 align:middle line:90%
don't move much.

01:18:16.280 --> 01:18:18.130 align:middle line:84%
And then once you get
up to some threshold,

01:18:18.130 --> 01:18:19.480 align:middle line:84%
you move very
quickly, either back

01:18:19.480 --> 01:18:21.188 align:middle line:84%
into the range where
you don't learn much

01:18:21.188 --> 01:18:23.890 align:middle line:84%
or into your quality
is 1, and then

01:18:23.890 --> 01:18:25.245 align:middle line:90%
people all start going to you.

01:18:25.245 --> 01:18:32.070 align:middle line:90%


01:18:32.070 --> 01:18:34.230 align:middle line:84%
Consumer welfare
under any policy

01:18:34.230 --> 01:18:36.850 align:middle line:84%
is the W that arises
given the policy.

01:18:36.850 --> 01:18:41.160 align:middle line:84%
So consumer optimality, like
what's best for consumers

01:18:41.160 --> 01:18:42.660 align:middle line:84%
if you're trying
to-- if you're Yelp

01:18:42.660 --> 01:18:44.910 align:middle line:84%
and you're trying to
design my platform

01:18:44.910 --> 01:18:47.670 align:middle line:84%
to be consumer optimal so that
people will continue to use

01:18:47.670 --> 01:18:51.820 align:middle line:84%
Yelp, what's optimal
for the firm to do

01:18:51.820 --> 01:18:55.150 align:middle line:84%
is to give firms
the incentive to be

01:18:55.150 --> 01:18:59.220 align:middle line:84%
willing to price below cost to
have firms learn about them.

01:18:59.220 --> 01:19:01.620 align:middle line:84%
And so then what
do you get about--

01:19:01.620 --> 01:19:04.080 align:middle line:84%
what would be the
optimal policy if you're

01:19:04.080 --> 01:19:06.270 align:middle line:84%
trying to get these guys
to be willing to pay a lot

01:19:06.270 --> 01:19:09.270 align:middle line:90%
to get more reviews?

01:19:09.270 --> 01:19:11.700 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out
that the answer is

01:19:11.700 --> 01:19:15.870 align:middle line:84%
that you want to have a
partially revealing customer

01:19:15.870 --> 01:19:20.370 align:middle line:84%
policy where you don't
publish all the reviews

01:19:20.370 --> 01:19:22.190 align:middle line:90%
about high-quality firms.

01:19:22.190 --> 01:19:24.030 align:middle line:84%
What you want to do
is, in some sense,

01:19:24.030 --> 01:19:29.400 align:middle line:84%
as soon as a firm's
posterior passes p tilde,

01:19:29.400 --> 01:19:33.180 align:middle line:84%
you want to stop publishing any
reviews about them whatsoever.

01:19:33.180 --> 01:19:36.560 align:middle line:84%
And you do that
because that way,

01:19:36.560 --> 01:19:40.490 align:middle line:84%
by having a flat benefit
from being quality p tilde

01:19:40.490 --> 01:19:44.720 align:middle line:84%
or all the way up to here,
when you have a flatter

01:19:44.720 --> 01:19:48.080 align:middle line:84%
benefit from high
quality, there's

01:19:48.080 --> 01:19:52.118 align:middle line:84%
greater incentive to make the
jump from here up to here.

01:19:52.118 --> 01:19:54.410 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, these firms
are willing to price further

01:19:54.410 --> 01:19:57.110 align:middle line:84%
below cost in order
to make that jump.

01:19:57.110 --> 01:19:59.520 align:middle line:84%
And then you are
disadvantaging some firms.

01:19:59.520 --> 01:20:02.390 align:middle line:84%
You're hurting firms up
here, helping firms down here

01:20:02.390 --> 01:20:05.030 align:middle line:84%
by not promoting-- providing
quality information on them that

01:20:05.030 --> 01:20:07.940 align:middle line:84%
lets them show that
they're really good.

01:20:07.940 --> 01:20:11.000 align:middle line:84%
But since new
firms are much more

01:20:11.000 --> 01:20:14.120 align:middle line:84%
likely to be here than they are
to be here, when you're here,

01:20:14.120 --> 01:20:15.780 align:middle line:84%
you're willing to
pay more to get up.

01:20:15.780 --> 01:20:18.560 align:middle line:84%
If you take some of the surplus
away from the really successful

01:20:18.560 --> 01:20:21.650 align:middle line:84%
firms and give it to the
moderately successful firms,

01:20:21.650 --> 01:20:24.750 align:middle line:84%
that raises the incentive
to price in that way.

01:20:24.750 --> 01:20:29.140 align:middle line:84%
So what he argues is that
what a platform would like

01:20:29.140 --> 01:20:30.940 align:middle line:84%
to do in this model
is to actually

01:20:30.940 --> 01:20:33.790 align:middle line:84%
give incomplete information
about restaurants

01:20:33.790 --> 01:20:35.890 align:middle line:84%
and to selectively
give information,

01:20:35.890 --> 01:20:40.180 align:middle line:84%
where we restrict information
about the good products so

01:20:40.180 --> 01:20:42.930 align:middle line:90%
that we have more--

01:20:42.930 --> 01:20:44.400 align:middle line:84%
so that there's
more of a benefit

01:20:44.400 --> 01:20:46.440 align:middle line:84%
to having a pretty
good reputation so

01:20:46.440 --> 01:20:48.120 align:middle line:84%
that new firms will
then invest more

01:20:48.120 --> 01:20:50.215 align:middle line:84%
in trying to get the
pretty good reputation.

01:20:50.215 --> 01:20:51.840 align:middle line:84%
And then obviously,
all the other firms

01:20:51.840 --> 01:20:55.955 align:middle line:84%
are pricing off half the price
to outcompete those guys.

01:20:55.955 --> 01:20:59.570 align:middle line:90%


01:20:59.570 --> 01:21:00.843 align:middle line:90%
OK, that is it.

01:21:00.843 --> 01:21:02.010 align:middle line:90%
That's what I had for today.

01:21:02.010 --> 01:21:07.430 align:middle line:84%
So Wednesday, I'm going to talk
about empirical work on entry.

01:21:07.430 --> 01:21:10.970 align:middle line:84%
I would say Bresnahan,
Reiss is really

01:21:10.970 --> 01:21:13.280 align:middle line:90%
the classic paper on entry.

01:21:13.280 --> 01:21:16.370 align:middle line:84%
Again, Bresnahan, Reiss
is a little old by now,

01:21:16.370 --> 01:21:19.130 align:middle line:84%
so I would say that if
you want to read things--

01:21:19.130 --> 01:21:21.020 align:middle line:84%
I will tell you what's
in Bresnahan and Reiss

01:21:21.020 --> 01:21:23.600 align:middle line:84%
with slightly more
modern econometrics

01:21:23.600 --> 01:21:24.560 align:middle line:90%
or what would be in it.

01:21:24.560 --> 01:21:27.710 align:middle line:84%
And then maybe read
Berry, Waldfogel, or look

01:21:27.710 --> 01:21:30.785 align:middle line:84%
at one of the Bronnenberg,
maybe Bronnenberg, Dhar,

01:21:30.785 --> 01:21:32.240 align:middle line:90%
and Dubé or whatever.

01:21:32.240 --> 01:21:34.960 align:middle line:84%
But I'll talk about
those four papers.

01:21:34.960 --> 01:21:48.000 align:middle line:90%